d Zhao are both taller than Engineer, which would mean Engineer is shorter than both. But if Engineer is Zhao, then Zhao is the engineer, and both Wang and Zhao are taller than Engineer, which would mean Engineer is shorter than Zhao, which is impossible because Zhao is the engineer. So that can't be.\n\nSimilarly, if Engineer is Wang, then Wang is the engineer, but then Zhao is taller than Engineer (Wang), which would mean Zhao > Wang. But then the secretary is shorter than Wang, so Secretary < Wang. So the order would be Zhao > Wang (Engineer) > Secretary. But then the secretary would have to be Zhang, because the roles are director, secretary, engineer. So Engineer is Wang, Secretary is Zhang, and Director is Zhao. But then, Zhang (secretary) and Zhang (secretary) are the same, which is fine, but we also have that Zhang and secretary are not the same height. Wait, no, the statement is \"Zhang and secretary are not the same height.\" So if Zhang is the secretary, then Zhang and secretary are the same person, which would mean their heights are the same, but the statement says they are not the same height. Therefore, this is impossible. So Engineer cannot be Wang.\n\nTherefore, Engineer must be Zhang.\n\nSo, the roles are:\n\n- Engineer: Zhang\n- Secretary: Zhao\n- Director: Wang\n\nWhich is option B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) Zhang is the engineer, Wang is the director, and Zhao is the secretary.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
