      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option best supports the given discussion about how fever promotes the transfer of lymphocytes to the site of infection. Let me break down the information provided.\n\nThe context says that fever increases the expression of Hsp90 in T lymphocytes. Hsp90 then binds to integrins, which causes the T cells to adhere to blood vessels and migrate faster to the infection site. So the key points are: fever \u2192 Hsp90 expression \u2192 Hsp90 binds integrins \u2192 T cells adhere and migrate.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: Integrin is a cell adhesion molecule that can control the transport of T lymphocytes during fever. This seems relevant because integrins are involved in adhesion, which is part of the process. But does it directly support the mechanism involving Hsp90? It mentions integrin's role but doesn't tie it to Hsp90.\n\nOption B: Fever can induce Hsp90 to bind to the tail of integrin and activate integrin. This directly connects fever, Hsp90, and integrin activation. It supports the idea that Hsp90 binding to integrin is what happens during fever, which aligns with the context.\n\nOption C: Not only fever, other stress can also induce Hsp90 expression. While this is true, it doesn't specifically support the fever-induced process described. It's more about other factors, which isn't the focus here.\n\nOption D: After Hsp90 binds to integrin, it activates a signaling pathway promoting migration. This also seems relevant because it explains the next step after binding\u2014activating a pathway that leads to migration. It supports the mechanism by showing the outcome of the binding.\n\nSo, both B and D seem to support the discussion. But the question is asking which can best support the discussion. The context mentions that Hsp90 binds to integrins, leading to adhesion and migration. Option B specifically talks about how fever induces Hsp90 to bind integrin and activate it, which directly ties fever to the mechanism. Option D explains the result of the binding but doesn't mention fever's role in initiating it.\n\nTherefore, the best support comes from Option B because it directly connects fever to the activation of integrin via Hsp90, which is central to the discussion.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
