one of the other five is in two sub-committees, and the remaining four are in one each.\n\nWait, but the problem says each member is in at least one sub-committee, so that's fine.\n\nNow, let's consider the constraints.\n\nFirst, F is not in the same sub-committee as G. So F and G cannot be together in any sub-committee. Similarly, H and I cannot be in the same sub-committee.\n\nNow, since one person is in all three sub-committees, let's call that person X. X is in all three sub-committees. So each sub-committee has X plus two others.\n\nSo each sub-committee is X plus two other members. Now, considering the constraints, we need to arrange the other members such that F and G are never together, and H and I are never together.\n\nLet me try to model this.\n\nLet's denote the three sub-committees as S1, S2, S3.\n\nEach S has X plus two others.\n\nSo S1: X, A, B\n\nS2: X, C, D\n\nS3: X, E, F\n\nWait, but the members are F, G, H, I, M, P. So X could be any of them, but let's see.\n\nWait, but F is a member, so if X is F, then F is in all three sub-committees. But then, in each sub-committee, F is present, so G cannot be in any sub-committee with F. But since F is in all three, G cannot be in any sub-committee, which contradicts the fact that each member is in at least one sub-committee. So X cannot be F because G would have nowhere to go. Similarly, if X is G, then F cannot be in any sub-committee, which is impossible. So X cannot be F or G.\n\nSimilarly, let's check H and I. If X is H, then I cannot be in any sub-committee, which is impossible. Similarly, if X is I, H cannot be in any sub-committee. So X cannot be H or I either.\n\nSo the only possible candidates for X are M or P.\n\nSo X is either M or P.\n\nLet's consider X as M first.\n\nSo each sub-committee has M plus two others.\n\nNow, we have to assign the other members: F, G, H, I, P.\n\nWe need to arrange them into the three sub-committees, each with two members, such that F and G are never together, and H and I are never together.\n\nLet me try to assign.\n\nSub-committee 1: M, F, H\n\nSub-committee 2: M, G, I\n\nSub-committee 3: M, P, (but wait, we have only F, G, H, I, P left. Wait, in this case, Sub-committee 3 would need two more members besides M. Let's see:\n\nWait, if X is M, then each sub-committee is M plus two others. So we have to distribute F, G, H, I, P into the three sub-committees, each taking two, but ensuring F and G are not together, and H and I are not together.\n\nLet me try:\n\nSub1: M, F, H\n\nSub2: M, G, I\n\nSub3: M, P, (but we have only five members besides M, so Sub3 would be M, P, and one more. Wait, but we've already used F, G, H, I in Sub1 and Sub2. So Sub3 would be M, P, and... but we've only five members, so perhaps Sub3 is M, P, and one more, but that would require that one member is in two sub-committees. Wait, but we have to assign all five members into the three sub-committees, each taking two, so that's 3*2=6 slots, but we have five members, so one member must be in two sub-committees.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that one of the other five is in two sub-committees, but in this case, since X is M, and each sub-committee has M plus two others, the other five members (F, G, H, I, P) must be distributed into the three sub-committees, each taking two, so that's 6 slots, but we have five members, so one member must be in two sub-committees, and the others in one each.\n\nSo let's try:\n\nSub1: M, F, H\n\nSub2: M, G, I\n\nSub3: M, P, F\n\nWait, but then F is in Sub1 and Sub3, which is allowed because F is only restricted from being with G. But in Sub3, F is with P and M, which is fine. However, in Sub1, F is with H, which is okay because H and I are not together. Wait, but in Sub2, G is with I, which is allowed because H and I are not together, but wait, H is in Sub1, I in Sub2, so that's fine.\n\nWait, but in this case, F is in two sub-committees, which is allowed. So this arrangement works.\n\nBut let's check the constraints:\n\n- F is