    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is an example of undifferentiated marketing based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding what undifferentiated marketing means. From the context, it says that undifferentiated marketing is when companies don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. Instead, they focus on the commonalities of the market as a whole. They launch a single product and use a single marketing mix to try to meet the needs of as many customers as possible to a certain extent.\n\nSo, the key points here are: single product, single marketing mix, not targeting specific sub-markets, and trying to appeal to a broad market.\n\nNow, let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: A cosmetic company found that there's a gap in the market for middle and low-grade whitening cosmetics. They developed a whitening product and promoted it. Hmm, this seems like they identified a specific need (whitening) in a particular segment (middle and low grade). So they're targeting a specific sub-market, which would be differentiated marketing, not undifferentiated. So A is probably not the answer.\n\nOption B: An enterprise saw that others were making profits with health care products, so they imitated them. This sounds like they're copying others' products, but it doesn't specify if they're targeting a specific market or just making similar products. However, the key here is that they're imitating, which might mean they're not focusing on a specific sub-market but just following the trend. But I'm not sure if this is undifferentiated. It might be more about copying rather than targeting a broad market. I'll keep this in mind but might not choose B.\n\nOption C: A multinational company produces only one beverage and uses the same packaging and advertising worldwide. This fits the definition because they're not changing their product or marketing for different regions. They're using a single product and same approach everywhere, ignoring the specific characteristics of each sub-market. So this seems like undifferentiated marketing.\n\nOption D: An enterprise found that people in another country also like instant coffee, so they sold the same coffee there as in China, same packaging and price. This is interesting because they're adjusting their sales to a different market but keeping the product and marketing the same. So they're not changing anything for the new market, just selling the same product. This also seems like undifferentiated because they're not tailoring to the specific needs of country A beyond just selling the same product.\n\nWait, but the definition says they don't consider sub-markets. So in option C, they're ignoring all sub-markets and just using one product globally. In option D, they are considering that people in country A like instant coffee, which is a specific sub-market, but they're not changing the product; they're just selling the same one there. So is D undifferentiated?\n\nHmm, the definition says they don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. So if they do consider a sub-market (like country A liking instant coffee) but still don't change the product, is that undifferentiated? Or is it still undifferentiated because they're not tailoring the product to that sub-market's specific needs beyond just selling the same product.\n\nWait, the key is whether they're considering the sub-markets. If they found that country A likes instant coffee, that's considering a sub-market, but they're not changing the product. So they're still using a single product for multiple sub-markets, which might still be undifferentiated. But I'm a bit confused here.\n\nLet me think again. Undifferentiated marketing is when you don't consider sub-markets and just focus on the commonalities. So if a company finds that a sub-market exists (like country A liking instant coffee) and decides to sell the same product there, are they considering the sub-market? Or are they just using the same product regardless.\n\nIn option C, they don't consider any sub-markets and just use one product everywhere. In option D, they do consider a sub-market (country A) but still use the same product. So maybe D is still undifferentiated because they're not tailoring the product to that sub-market, just selling the same one. But I'm not entirely sure.\n\nWait, the definition says they don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. So if they do consider a sub-market, even if they don't change the product, is that still undifferentiated? Or does considering the sub-market make it differentiated?\n\nI think the key is whether they're targeting specific sub-markets. If they're targeting a specific sub-market but not changing the product, it's still undifferentiated because they're not tailoring. But if they're not considering sub-markets at all, that's also undifferentiated.\n\nSo in option C, they're not considering any sub-markets, just using one product everywhere. In option D, they are considering a sub-market (country A) but still using the same product. So both C and D might be undifferentiated, but the question is asking for which one is an example.\n\nWait, but the definition says they don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. So if they do consider a sub-market, even if they don't change the product, they're still considering it, which might make it not undifferentiated. Because undifferentiated is when you don't consider sub-markets at all.\n\nSo in option D, they did consider a sub-market (country A) and adjusted their sales strategy, but kept the product the same. So that might be a form of differentiated marketing because they're targeting a specific sub-market, even if they're not changing the product. Or maybe not, because they're not changing the product.\n\nI'm getting a bit confused here. Let me try to clarify.\n\nUndifferentiated marketing: one product, same marketing mix, no consideration of sub-markets.\n\nDifferentiated marketing: multiple products or marketing mixes tailored to different sub-markets.\n\nConcentrated marketing: focus on one specific sub-market.\n\nSo in option C, the company is using one product globally, not considering any sub-markets. That's undifferentiated.\n\nIn option D, the company found a specific sub-market (country A) and sold the same product there. So they are considering a sub-market but not changing the product. Is that undifferentiated or not?\n\nI think it's still undifferentiated because they're not tailoring the product to that sub-market. They're just selling the same product in a new sub-market. So they're not considering the specific characteristics beyond just the fact that people there like instant coffee. They're not changing the product to meet any specific needs of country A beyond that.\n\nWait, but the definition says they don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. So if they do consider a sub-market, even if they don't change the product, they're still considering it, which would make it not undifferentiated.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Maybe I should look for the option where the company doesn't consider any sub-markets at all.\n\nOption C: They don't consider any sub-markets, just one product everywhere. That's undifferentiated.\n\nOption D: They do consider a sub-market (country A) and adjust their sales, but keep the product the same. So they are considering a sub-market, which might make it not undifferentiated.\n\nWait, but the definition says they don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. So if they do consider a sub-market, even if they don't change the product, they're still considering it, which would mean it's not undifferentiated.\n\nSo in that case, option C is the correct answer because they don't consider any sub-markets, while option D does consider a sub-market, even if they don't change the product.\n\nWait, but the definition says they don't consider the characteristics of each sub-market. So if they consider a sub-market, even if they don't change the product, they're still considering it, which would make it not undifferentiated.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
