ithout apartments. But the argument is about apartments, so maybe this isn't the main issue.\n\nB) Talks about other types of rental units, but the argument is about apartments, so maybe not.\n\nC) The conclusion doesn't restate a premise, so probably not.\n\nD) Fails to address that some houses might have three or more. But the main issue is that some might have only one, which would make the conclusion that \"most\" have more than one incorrect.\n\nWait, but none of the options directly address the possibility that some houses have only one apartment. Let me check the options again.\n\nOption A: Overlooks the possibility that some buildings are not old houses. But the first premise says all apartments are in old houses, so any building with an apartment is an old house. But maybe there are other buildings on 20th Ave that are not old houses, but the argument doesn't mention them. However, the argument is about old houses and apartments, so maybe this isn't the main flaw.\n\nOption B: Fails to consider other types of rental accommodation. The argument is about apartments, so maybe this isn't the main issue.\n\nOption C: Conclusion restates a premise. No, the conclusion is a new statement.\n\nOption D: Fails to address that some houses might have three or more. But the main issue is that some might have only one, which would mean that the conclusion that \"most\" have more than one is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but none of the options directly address the possibility that some houses have only one apartment. So maybe I'm missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps the flaw is that the argument assumes that all old houses have at least one apartment, but that's not necessarily the case. The first premise says all apartments are in old houses, but it doesn't say that all old houses have apartments. So, there could be old houses without any apartments, which would mean that the number of old houses is more than the number of apartments, but the premise says there are twice