      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem about six switches: S, T, W, X, Y, and Z. The question is, given that both S and Z are turned on, which of the options A to D must be true. Let me break down the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll list out all the given conditions to make sure I don't miss any:\n\n1. If W is turned on, then X should also be turned on. So, W \u2192 X.\n2. Only when S is turned off can T also be turned off. So, if S is on, T must be on. Because if S is off, T can be off, but if S is on, T can't be off. So, \u00acS \u2192 \u00acT, but the contrapositive would be T \u2192 S. Wait, no, actually, the condition says that T can be off only if S is off. So, if S is on, T must be on. So, S \u2192 T.\n3. T and X cannot be both on or both off. So, T and X are always in opposite states. So, T \u2194 \u00acX. That means if T is on, X is off, and vice versa.\n4. If Y and Z are both on, then W must also be on. So, Y \u2227 Z \u2192 W.\n\nNow, the given information is that S and Z are both on. So, S = on, Z = on.\n\nLet me go through each condition one by one with this information.\n\nStarting with condition 2: Since S is on, from S \u2192 T, T must be on. So, T is on.\n\nCondition 3: Since T is on, from T \u2194 \u00acX, X must be off. So, X is off.\n\nCondition 4: Since Z is on, and we don't know Y yet, but if Y is on, then because Z is on, W must be on. So, Y \u2227 Z \u2192 W. But we don't know Y's state yet.\n\nCondition 1: If W is on, then X must be on. But we already determined that X is off. So, if W were on, X would have to be on, which contradicts our earlier conclusion. Therefore, W cannot be on. So, W must be off.\n\nWait, that's an important point. Since X is off, and condition 1 says W \u2192 X, the contrapositive is \u00acX \u2192 \u00acW. So, if X is off, W must be off. So, W is off.\n\nNow, going back to condition 4: Y and Z are both on \u2192 W is on. But we just found that W is off. So, the antecedent (Y \u2227 Z) must be false because the implication is true only if either the antecedent is false or the consequent is true. Since W is off, the consequent is false, so the antecedent must be false. Therefore, Y \u2227 Z is false. Since Z is on, Y must be off. Because if Z is on, for Y \u2227 Z to be false, Y must be off. So, Y is off.\n\nSo, summarizing what we have so far:\n\nS = on\n\nZ = on\n\nT = on (from condition 2)\n\nX = off (from condition 3)\n\nW = off (from condition 1 and X being off)\n\nY = off (from condition 4 and Z being on)\n\nNow, let's look at the options:\n\nA) T is turned on and Y is turned off. We have T on and Y off. So, A is true.\n\nB) Both T and Y are turned off. But T is on, so B is false.\n\nC) Both W and T are turned on. W is off, so C is false.\n\nD) X is turned on and Y is turned off. X is off, so D is false.\n\nSo, the only correct option is A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
