g ones will stay operational. But does this directly tie into the reasoning about the number matching the size and current numbers? Maybe, but I'm not sure if it's the strongest option.\n\nC) The number of primary school-age children in this area will not increase much in the future. This directly relates to future needs. If the number of students isn't expected to grow, then the current buses are sufficient. So this assumption supports the department's decision because it implies that future demand won't exceed current capacity.\n\nD) The education administrative department has no plan to expand the scale of teachers and students of the school. This is about the school's size not increasing. If the school isn't planning to grow, then the current buses are enough. This also directly supports the decision because it ties into the reasoning that the number of buses matches the current size and numbers.\n\nNow, I need to decide between options C and D because both seem relevant. The department's reasoning was about the current size and numbers, so an assumption about future growth would support their decision. \n\nOption C talks about the number of children not increasing much, which affects the number of students. Option D talks about the school not expanding in terms of teachers and students, which is exactly what the department mentioned.\n\nSo, D seems more directly related because it's about the school's scale not expanding, which is exactly what the department's reasoning was based on. If the school isn't expanding, then the current buses are sufficient, supporting their decision to reject the application.\n\nWait, but the department's reasoning was that the current buses are enough based on the current size and numbers. So, if the school isn't planning to expand, that means the current size and numbers won't change, so the buses remain sufficient. That makes D a strong assumption.\n\nOn the other hand, C is about the number of children not increasing, which would affect the number of students. If the number of children isn't increasing, then the school's student population won't grow, so the buses are sufficient. But the department's reasoning was about the current size and numbers, not necessarily future projections. However, the question is about the assumption that supports their decision, which might involve future considerations.\n\nWait, the department's decision was based on the current situation, but the assumption could be about the future to justify why they don't need more buses now. So, if they assume that the school won't expand, then the current buses are enough. Similarly, if they assume the number of children won't increase, that also justifies the current buses being sufficient.\n\nBut the department's reasoning specifically mentioned the current number and scale, so perhaps the assumption is about the future not changing that. So, if the school isn't planning to expand, then the current buses are enough. That makes D the correct assumption.\n\nAlternatively, if the number of children isn't increasing, then the school's student body won't grow, so the buses are sufficient. But the department's reasoning was about the current size and numbers, so maybe the assumption is about the future not changing that. So, D is about the school's scale not expanding, which directly ties into their reasoning.\n\nI think I'm a bit torn between C and D, but I think D is the better option because it's about the school's scale not expanding, which is exactly what the department's reasoning was based on. The department said the current buses are enough because of the current size and numbers, so if the school isn't planning to expand, that supports their decision.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
