      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through it again to make sure I understand what's being asked.\n\nThe context is a meteorologist's argument. They say that the number of tornadoes reported each year has more than doubled since the 1950s. But they don't think the actual number of tornadoes has increased. Instead, they believe it's because our ability to detect tornadoes has improved, so we're just finding more of them than before.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options, if true, would best support this argument. So I need to find the option that provides the most support for the idea that the increase in reported tornadoes is due to better detection, not an actual increase in tornadoes.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: The annual number of deaths due to tornadoes has increased steadily since the 1950s. Hmm, if deaths have increased, that might suggest that either there are more tornadoes or that they're more deadly. But the meteorologist's argument is about detection, not the severity or impact. So I'm not sure if this directly supports the argument. It could be a factor, but maybe not the strongest support.\n\nOption B: The number of tornadoes hitting major population centers annually has more than doubled since the 1950s. This seems related because if more tornadoes are hitting cities, that might mean more reports. But wait, the meteorologist is saying that the actual number hasn't increased, so if hitting population centers has doubled, that might imply that detection is better because we're noticing more in areas where people are. But I'm not sure if this is the best support. It could be, but let me think more.\n\nOption C: The geographic range in which tornadoes are most prevalent has remained roughly constant since the 1950s. This is interesting. If the area where tornadoes occur hasn't changed, but the number reported has increased, that suggests that we're just finding more