cause it's a negative factor that doesn't explain the return.\n\nAlternatively, B is about people returning to small cities, which is the opposite, so it doesn't explain the return to big cities.\n\nI think I'm getting stuck here. Let me try to approach it differently.\n\nThe phenomenon is that people are returning to big cities. So the options that explain this would be positive factors about big cities. A and D are positive factors (lower housing prices, better infrastructure), so they explain the return.\n\nOptions B and C are negative factors. B is about people not being fit for big cities, which would explain why they left, not why they returned. C is about the difficulty in big cities, which might not explain the return.\n\nSo the question is which option does not account for the phenomenon. So the correct answer would be the one that doesn't explain the return.\n\nBetween B and C, B is about people returning to small cities, which is the opposite, so it doesn't explain the return to big cities. C is about difficulty in big cities, which might not explain the return but could be a reason why they left.\n\nWait, but the question is about why they are returning. So if C is true, it's a negative factor, but people are still returning, so C doesn't account for the phenomenon because it's a reason why they wouldn't return.\n\nAlternatively, B is about people not being fit for big cities, so they return to small cities, which is the opposite of the phenomenon.\n\nSo the correct answer is B because it doesn't explain why people are returning to big cities; instead, it explains why they might leave.\n\nWait, but the question is which option does not account for the phenomenon. So if B is true, it doesn't explain the return to big cities because it's about people leaving big cities.\n\nSo the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
