      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is an example of bigamy based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding what bigamy is. The context says bigamy is when someone has a spouse and marries another person, or marries someone knowing that person has a spouse. So, it's about having two marriages at the same time without being legally free from the first.\n\nNow, let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Li Xiaoqing was engaged to Wang Gang, accepted gifts, but then tore up the contract and married a foreign man. Wait, engagement isn't a marriage. So, she wasn't legally married to Wang Gang. She just broke off the engagement. So, she didn't have a spouse when she married the foreign man. Therefore, this isn't bigamy.\n\nOption B: Huang was mistreated by her husband and fell in love with a colleague. They traveled together secretly. Her husband sued her for bigamy, but Huang said her real husband was the colleague. Hmm, so if Huang was legally married to her first husband, and she started a relationship with her colleague, but did she marry him? The option says they sometimes traveled together, but it doesn't mention a marriage. So, unless she actually married the colleague, it's not bigamy. She might be cheating, but not bigamy. Unless she got married without divorcing, but the option doesn't specify that. So, maybe not bigamy.\n\nOption C: A is married and has a son. He was abducted, sold to Qiu, and became her wife without registration. So, A is already married, and then he's forced into another marriage without registration. But since the first marriage is still valid, and he's now in another union, even if it's not registered, does that count? The definition says \"marrying someone knowing they have a spouse.\" Here, A was already married, so if he married Qiu, even without registration, it's bigamy. But wait, he was abducted and sold, so maybe he didn't consent. Does bigamy require intent? The definition says \"marrying someone knowing he or she has a spouse.\" If A was forced into the marriage, maybe he didn't know or consent. So, perhaps this isn't bigamy because it's not voluntary.\n\nOption D: Sun married Zhou, then decided to divorce due to personality differences. Zhou's parents took her home. Half a year later, Sun married another woman without going through the divorce. Zhou also married someone else. So, Sun was still legally married to Zhou when he married another woman. That's bigamy because he had a spouse and married again. Similarly, Zhou might have remarried without divorcing, but the question is about which option is bigamy. So, Sun's action is definitely bigamy.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which of the options is bigamy. So, looking back, option D describes Sun remarrying without divorcing, which fits the definition. Option C might be a case of bigamy if A consented, but since he was abducted, maybe not. Option B doesn't mention marriage, just a relationship. Option A was just an engagement. So, the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
