e. Option A is also good but perhaps less direct because it's a different organ.\n\nAlternatively, option B is about the feasibility of raising body temperature, which would make the suggested methods ineffective, thus refuting the assertion's solution.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit torn. Let me think about what each option does.\n\nThe assertion is that high body temperature can fight cancer because cancer-prone organs have low temperature. So the argument is based on two points: 1) cancer-prone organs have low temperature, and 2) high temperature can fight cancer.\n\nOption B refutes point 1 by saying that all organs have similar temperatures, so the premise is wrong.\n\nOption D refutes point 1 by providing an alternative reason for the heart's low cancer rate.\n\nOption A refutes point 1 by showing that a warmer organ has higher cancer incidence.\n\nSo all three (A, B, D) attack the premise. But which is the best?\n\nI think option B is the strongest because it undermines the entire premise that different organs have different temperatures. If all organs are roughly the same temperature, then the initial assertion that cancer-prone organs are colder is incorrect.\n\nOption D is also strong because it explains why the heart doesn't get cancer, which was part of the assertion's example.\n\nBut the question is about which option, if true, can best refute the assertion. So perhaps option B is the best because it invalidates the entire premise about organ temperatures.\n\nAlternatively, option D is more specific to the example given, making it a more direct refutation.\n\nI think I need to decide between B and D.\n\nThe assertion uses the heart as an example of a high-temperature organ not getting cancer. If option D says it's not because of temperature but because the cells don't divide, that directly refutes the assertion's reasoning.\n\nOption B, on the other hand, says that all organs have similar temperatures, which would mean that the initial premise is wrong, but it's a more general statement.\n\nI think D is more specific and directly addresses the example, making it a better refutation.\n\nWait, but the assertion is that high body temperature can fight cancer, so if the body can't raise its temperature (as per B), then the method suggested is ineffective. So B would also be a good refutation because it shows that the method won't work.\n\nSo now I'm considering both B and D as strong options.\n\nBut the question is about refuting the assertion, which is that high body temperature can fight cancer because cancer-prone organs have low temperature.\n\nSo if B is true, then the premise that organs have varying temperatures is wrong, making the entire assertion invalid.\n\nIf D is true, it explains why the heart doesn't get cancer, which was part of the assertion's reasoning.\n\nI think B is a more fundamental refutation because it invalidates the premise that different organs have different temperatures, which is the basis of the assertion.\n\nSo perhaps B is the best answer.\n\nWait, but the assertion also suggests that you can raise your body temperature to fight cancer. If B is true, then the body's temperature is tightly regulated, so you can't really raise it, making the suggested methods ineffective. So B attacks both the premise and the solution.\n\nOption D attacks the premise by providing an alternative reason for the heart's low cancer rate.\n\nSo I think B is the best because it undermines both the premise and the solution.\n\nWait, but the question is about refuting the assertion, which is the claim that high body temperature can fight cancer because cancer-prone organs have low temperature.\n\nSo if B is true, then the premise that organs have varying temperatures is wrong, which directly refutes the assertion.\n\nOption D is also good but perhaps more specific.\n\nI think I need to choose between B and D.\n\nIn the options, B says that the body has precise temperature regulation, so no obvious temperature differences bet