nts are registered in the city. So, that means every third-grader is a student with city registration. Therefore, it's definitely true that some (in fact, all) third-grade students are registered here. So, C seems correct.\n\nOption D: Some first-grade students are not children of migrant workers. The context mentions that some first-grade students are children of migrant workers, but it doesn't say anything about whether all first-graders are or aren't. It's possible that some are and some aren't. However, the statement says \"some are not,\" which isn't directly supported by the context. The context only tells us that some are, not that some aren't. So, we can't be certain about D.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says some first-grade students are children of migrant workers, but it doesn't specify if all first-graders are or not. So, it's possible that all first-graders are children of migrant workers, but that's not stated. Therefore, we can't conclude that some are not. So, D might not be correct.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct option seems to be C because it's directly supported by the context that all third-graders are registered, which means some (actually all) students with city registration are third-graders.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
