      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option weakens the expert's opinion the most. Let me start by understanding the context. The experts think that because Antarctica is getting more snowfall, it's storing more frozen water, which might reverse the sea level rise caused by melting glaciers. So their main point is that increased snowfall in Antarctica is offsetting the melting ice elsewhere.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option, if true, would weaken this optimistic view. So I need to find the option that shows that the snowfall increase isn't enough to counteract the melting.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A says that snowfall in Antarctica has slightly decreased recently. If that's true, it directly contradicts the premise that snowfall has increased. But wait, the context says that over the past 100 years, snowfall has increased. If it's only slightly decreased in recent years, maybe the overall trend is still positive. So this might not be the strongest weakness because the long-term trend is still up, even if recent years have a slight dip.\n\nOption B states that the increase in ice from snowfall is only one-third of the ice lost from melting glaciers. That means the gain from snow is much less than the loss from melting. So even though Antarctica is gaining ice, it's not enough to offset the melting elsewhere. This directly weakens the expert's argument because it shows the net effect is still a loss of ice, leading to sea level rise.\n\nOption C suggests that researchers might have missed some causes of global warming. While that's a possibility, it doesn't directly address the snowfall and ice storage argument. It's more about the causes of global warming, which is a broader issue, not specifically about the balance between snowfall and melting.\n\nOption D mentions that this year's temperature might be slightly lower. If temperatures are lower, maybe melting is less, but the expert's argument is about the long-term trend. A single year's temperature change might not be significant enough to affect the overall trend of sea level rise. Plus, the context is about a 100-year trend, so a slight dip this year might not be enough to weaken the argument.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option B is the strongest because it directly compares the gain from snowfall to the loss from melting and shows that the loss is three times greater. This means the net effect is still a rise in sea levels, which weakens the expert's optimistic estimate.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
