      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. The context says that North America shifted its main energy sources over the past century. First, they moved from wood to coal, then from coal to oil and natural gas. Each time, the new fuel had less carbon and more hydrogen than the previous one. The argument is that in the future, the main energy source will be pure hydrogen.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options expresses a general principle that underlies this argument. So, I need to identify the underlying principle that the argument is based on.\n\nLet me break down the argument. The shifts are from wood to coal to oil and natural gas, each time with less carbon and more hydrogen. So, each transition involves a decrease in carbon and an increase in hydrogen. The conclusion is that the next step will be pure hydrogen, meaning all carbon is eliminated, and only hydrogen remains.\n\nLooking at the options, I need to find which one captures this idea. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"If each step in a series of changes involves a decrease of one attribute of the thing undergoing the change and an increase of another, the series will terminate with the first attribute eliminated and only the second attribute present.\" Hmm, this seems to fit. Each step decreases carbon and increases hydrogen, so the series would end with no carbon and only hydrogen. That matches the argument.\n\nOption B: \"If the second stage of a process has been completed more quickly than the first stage, the third stage of that process will be completed more quickly than the second stage.\" The context doesn't mention anything about the speed of the transitions, just the attributes changing. So this doesn't seem relevant.\n\nOption C: \"If each of two desirable attributes belongs to a useful substance, then the most useful form of that substance will have those two attributes in equal amounts.\" The argument doesn't talk about the desirability of attributes or equal amounts. It's about a trend of increasing hydrogen and decreasing carbon, not balancing them.\n\nOption D: \"If one substance is better for a certain purpose than another substance is, then the best substance for that purpose is one that includes among its attributes all of the attributes of the first substance and none of the attributes of the second substance.\" The argument doesn't compare substances as better or worse in terms of having all attributes of one and none of another. It's more about a trend of increasing one attribute and decreasing another.\n\nSo, Option A seems to directly capture the principle that each step reduces one attribute and increases another, leading to the elimination of the first and only the second remaining. That's exactly what the argument is suggesting with the energy sources moving towards pure hydrogen.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
