hat tyrosine stimulates the formation of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. But it doesn't say that fish themselves are rich in dopamine. They have tyrosine, which leads to dopamine production. So, fish aren't rich in dopamine; they have the building block for it. So C is probably incorrect.\n\nOption D: There is more fat in meat than in fish. The context mentions that meat contains tyrosine but fat interferes with its absorption. It doesn't explicitly say that meat has more fat than fish, but it does imply that meat has enough fat to interfere. Since fish are mentioned as having a lot of tyrosine, maybe they don't have as much fat. So, perhaps meat has more fat than fish. That seems like a possible inference.\n\nWait, but the context doesn't directly compare the fat content of meat and fish. It just says that meat's fat interferes with tyrosine absorption. So, maybe meat has more fat than fish, but I'm not entirely sure. Alternatively, maybe fish have less fat, so their tyrosine is absorbed better. But the context doesn't state that fish have less fat; it just says that meat's fat is a problem.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the only option that seems to be supported is D, because the context implies that meat has enough fat to interfere with tyrosine absorption, which suggests that meat has more fat than fish, which don't have this issue mentioned. So, D is the correct inference.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
