e's argument. The artist repaying benefits others (general benefits) and the value of those benefits (supporting other artists) is more important than the cost to the artist (repaying the subsidy).\n\nWait, but option A says others can get a benefit like the artist did. The passage says the returned money is a source of support for other artists, which is similar to the artist's initial benefit. So maybe A is also a contender.\n\nBut the passage's main point is that repaying helps others, not that others can get the same benefit. It's more about the flow of funds supporting others, not necessarily that others get the same subsidy. So D is about the general benefits outweighing the cost, which fits because the artist's repayment helps others, which is a general benefit.\n\nAlternatively, A is about others getting a similar benefit, which is a specific outcome. The passage is more about the system's sustainability, so D might be better.\n\nWait, but the passage says the money returned will be welcome as a source of support for other artists. So it's about others getting the same kind of benefit, which is a subsidy. So A might be correct because it's about others obtaining a benefit similar to what the artist received.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit confused now. Let me think again. The passage's argument is that repaying allows the subsidy agency to support others. So the artist's repayment leads to others getting subsidies. So the artist's action (repaying) allows others to get a benefit (subsidy) similar to what the artist received. So A is about others obtaining a benefit like the artist's past benefit.\n\nBut D is about providing general benefits that exceed the cost. The cost is the artist repaying, and the benefit is supporting others. So D is more about the overall value, not specifically that others get the same benefit.\n\nI think the passage is more about the fact that by repaying, the artist is enabling others to get subsidies, which is a similar benefit. So A might be the answer. But I'm not entirely sure.\n\nWait, the passage says the money returned will be welcome as a source of support for other artists. So it's about the returned money being used to help others, which is a general benefit. So D is about the general benefits outweighing the cost. The cost is the artist's repayment, and the benefit is the support for others, which is a general good.\n\nSo maybe D is the correct answer because it's about the overall benefit to society, not just others getting the same subsidy.\n\nI'm a bit torn between A and D. Let me read the options again.\n\nA) Acting this way would allow others to obtain a benefit such as the one that this artist has obtained in the past.\n\nD) This person, by acting this way, would provide general benefits with a value exceeding the cost to the person of acting this way.\n\nThe passage's argument is that repaying helps others, which is a general benefit. It's not specifically that others get the same subsidy but that the money is used to support others, which is a general benefit. So D seems to capture that idea better.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe passage argues that an artist should repay a subsidy because the returned funds support other artists, highlighting the general benefit to the system. This aligns with option D, which emphasizes the value of general benefits outweighing the cost of repayment.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
