      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem based on the given context about Mo Yan's literary works. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that Mo Yan's works have made the hidden Chinese vernacular known to readers worldwide and recognized by the international literary community. This has proven a certain judgment, which is stated as \"only the national is the world.\" Hmm, I need to understand what that means.\n\nSo, the judgment is \"only the national is the world.\" In logical terms, this sounds like a conditional statement. Let me parse it. \"Only the national is the world\" can be rephrased as \"If something is the world, then it must be national.\" In logical terms, that's like saying World \u2192 National. So, for something to be considered world literature, it has to be national first.\n\nNow, looking at the options, I need to see which one aligns with this judgment.\n\nOption A: \"It's national, it's world.\" That sounds like National \u2192 World. But wait, the original statement was World \u2192 National, not the other way around. So this might not be correct because the original doesn't say that being national guarantees being world, just that to be world, it must be national.\n\nOption B: \"If it is not national, it is not world.\" This is the contrapositive of the original statement. The original was World \u2192 National, so the contrapositive would be \u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld. Contrapositives are logically equivalent, so this should be correct.\n\nOption C: \"As long as the nation is, it is the world.\" This seems similar to Option A, implying National \u2192 World. Again, that's not what the original said. The original was about World requiring National, not the other way around.\n\nOption D: \"Only the world is national.\" That would mean National \u2192 World, which is the converse of the original statement. The original was World \u2192 National, so this is not equivalent.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The original judgment is \"only the national is the world,\" which is World \u2192 National. So the contrapositive is \u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld, which is Option B. So Option B should be correct.\n\nI think I got it. The correct answer is B because it's the contrapositive of the original statement, making it logically equivalent.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
