      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem, and I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D must be true based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nThe context says: Nearly all employees of the factory who have completed their high school diploma have earned their factory safety certificate. They were able to easily complete the eight-week training course. However, the high school graduates who haven't earned the certificate are those who have evening obligations that prevented them from taking the course. Overall, though, most of the factory workers have not earned the certificate.\n\nSo, breaking this down, there are a few key points:\n\n1. Most high school graduates (HS grads) have the certificate because they completed the training.\n2. The few HS grads without the certificate have evening obligations, so they couldn't take the course.\n3. Despite this, most factory workers overall don't have the certificate.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options must be true based on this information.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: A large proportion of the factory workers have not graduated from high school.\n\nHmm. The context says that nearly all HS grads have the certificate, but most workers don't have the certificate. So, if most workers don't have the certificate, and most HS grads do, maybe a lot of workers aren't HS grads. Because if they weren't HS grads, they might not have taken the course. But wait, the context doesn't say anything about non-HS grads. It only talks about HS grads and their certificate status. So, could it be that a large number of workers are non-HS grads? That might explain why most don't have the certificate. But is this necessarily true? The context doesn't explicitly state that non-HS grads can't get the certificate. It just says that HS grads who didn't have evening obligations got it. So, maybe some non-HS grads could have taken the course and gotten the certificate. But since most workers don't have it, perhaps a lot are non-HS grads. But I'm not sure if this is a must. It's possible, but not necessarily certain.\n\nOption B: A large proportion of students with high school diplomas have evening obligations that have prevented them from taking the eight-week training course.\n\nWait, the context says that the high school graduates who haven't earned the certificate are those with evening obligations. But it also says that nearly all HS grads have earned the certificate. So, the number of HS grads without the certificate is small. Therefore, the number of HS grads with evening obligations is small. So, option B says a large proportion have evening obligations, which contradicts the context. So B is not correct.\n\nOption C: Most of the workers who have earned the certificate are high school graduates.\n\nThe context says nearly all HS grads have the certificate. So, the majority of certificate holders are HS grads. Because if almost all HS grads have it, and the rest of the workers (non-HS grads) might have some, but if most workers don't have the certificate, then the certificate holders are mostly HS grads. So, C seems correct.\n\nOption D: No factory worker without a high school diploma has completed the course and earned the certificate.\n\nThe context doesn't say anything about non-HS grads. It only talks about HS grads. So, it's possible that some non-HS grads have taken the course and earned the certificate. The context doesn't exclude that possibility. So, D is not necessarily true.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- A: Possible, but not necessarily true.\n- B: Incorrect, because only a small number of HS grads have evening obligations.\n- C: Correct, because most certificate holders are HS grads.\n- D: Not necessarily true, as non-HS grads might have certificates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
