      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out the main content of the given text. Let me read through it carefully.\n\nThe text starts by talking about logic coming from a priori, meaning it exists before experience. It mentions that logic transcends experience and is eternal. The example given is the sum of interior angles of triangles being 180 degrees, which is a mathematical truth. The author seems to be pointing out that logical forms have self-consistency and correctness without relying on experience. \n\nThen, the text questions how humans, who are empirical beings, can know that such transcendental logic exists. It suggests that acknowledging this might lead to dogmatism or mysticism. So, the author is raising doubts about the existence of logic that's beyond experience.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Questioning the existence of transcendental logic\nB) It shows that mysticism is undesirable\nC) Argumentation logic is a summary of experience\nD) Explaining the objective world is sometimes illogical\n\nThe text is mainly about whether transcendental logic exists, especially since humans are empirical. It's not directly about mysticism being bad (B), nor is it saying logic is a summary of experience (C). It's also not about the objective world being illogical (D). So, the main point is questioning the existence of logic that's beyond experience, which is option A.\n</think>\n\nThe text discusses the concept of transcendental logic, questioning how humans, as empirical beings, can know such logic exists without falling into dogmatism or mysticism. It primarily focuses on the existence of logic beyond experience.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
