of those trees. Wait, the argument is that now it's cooler than then. So if the climate has never been warmer than that time, then the current climate is cooler. But does the argument assume that? Or is it possible that the climate was even warmer at some other time? I'm not sure. The argument is specifically comparing now to 10,000 years ago, so maybe B is not the assumption because the argument doesn't make a claim about other times, just that now is cooler than then.\n\nOption C: The fossils are not of species that could tolerate cooler temperatures than current species near the timberline. So this is saying that those trees couldn't handle cooler temperatures. If that's true, then the fact that they grew higher implies that the temperature was warmer. If the assumption were that those trees could handle cooler temps, then maybe they could have grown higher even if it was cooler, which would weaken the argument. So the argument must assume that those trees couldn't handle cooler temps, making C a necessary assumption.\n\nOption D: No trees grew 10,000 years ago at altitudes higher than where the fossils were found. So this is saying that the fossils are the highest trees from that time. If that's not the case, maybe there were trees even higher, but their fossils weren't found. But the argument is based on the fact that these trees were 100 meters above the current timberline. If there were higher trees, that would support the argument even more, but the argument doesn't rely on there being no higher trees. It just uses the found fossils as evidence. So D might not be necessary.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The argument says that because trees were found 100 meters above the current timberline, the climate was warmer then. So the assumption is that those trees couldn't grow at that altitude now because it's too cold. So the argument assumes that the trees in the fossils couldn't tolerate the cooler temperatures that the current timberline represents. That's exactly what option C is saying. So without assuming C, the argument wouldn't hold because maybe those trees could have grown in cooler temps, making the current timberline lower for a different reason, not because it's colder.\n\nSo I think the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument concludes that the Rocky Mountains' climate is cooler now than 10,000 years ago based on fossilized trees found above the current timberline. The key assumption is that these trees couldn't tolerate cooler temperatures, supporting the inference that the past climate was warmer. \n\nThe correct option is: C",
