idea that the population is declining because each out-mover might represent a family, while each in-mover is just one person. So this could strengthen the argument.\n\nOption C: Over the past century, any census showing a population loss was followed ten years later by a population gain. This seems to suggest a cyclical pattern where population goes down and then up every ten years. If that's the case, then even if the current survey shows a decline, the next census might show an increase. This would actually weaken the argument because it suggests that the decline might not be permanent and that the population could recover. So this doesn't help strengthen the argument.\n\nOption D: Most adults moving out were parents with children, while those staying or moving in were older people living alone. This is similar to option B but more specific. If the people moving out are parents with children, that means each household moving out has more people (like a family of four, for example). Meanwhile, the people moving in are older adults living alone, so each in-mover adds only one person. So the net effect is that for every two households leaving (each with, say, four people), only one household is coming in (one person). That would result in a significant population decline. This directly supports the argument because it shows that the number of people leaving is much higher than those coming in, even though the survey only shows a two-to-one ratio of households.\n\nSo, comparing options B and D, both seem relevant, but D is more specific about the number of people per household. Option B talks about age groups and job status, which might not directly translate to population numbers as clearly as D does. Option D directly ties the number of people moving out (families) versus those moving in (single adults), which would lead to a larger population decrease.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be D because it provides a reason why the household ratio leads to a population decline, considering the number of people per household.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
