      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option strengthens the essayist's argument. Let me start by understanding the argument first.\n\nThe essayist says that some researchers are criticizing British security agencies for not releasing enough UFO information. When civilian researchers ask for data, they get brushed off. This makes the essayist think that there must be extraterrestrial spacecraft near Earth because the government isn't sharing info.\n\nSo the argument is: lack of information release \u2192 implies something is being hidden \u2192 which suggests UFOs are real and extraterrestrial.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option, if true, would strengthen this argument. So I need to find the option that supports the idea that the government's secrecy implies UFOs are real.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: The British government would deny requests only if they had something to hide. Hmm, this seems relevant. If the government only hides info when they have something to hide, and they are hiding UFO info, then it suggests they have something to hide, which supports the essayist's suspicion.\n\nOption B: The government is less trusting of civilian researchers than military ones. This might explain why they deny requests, but it doesn't directly tie to UFOs being extraterrestrial. It just explains the denial, not the reason behind it.\n\nOption C: The government withholds info only if UFOs are from other planets. Wait, that's a bit circular. If they only withhold info when UFOs are extraterrestrial, then withholding implies they are. But does this strengthen the argument? It seems to directly link the withholding to the conclusion the essayist wants.\n\nOption D: The government has always denied UFO existence. This might weaken the argument because if they deny existence, it doesn't necessarily mean they're hiding something extraterrestrial. It could mean they just don't believe in UFOs at all.\n\nSo, between A and C. Let me think again. The essayist's argument is that the government's secrecy suggests UFOs are real and extraterrestrial. So, if the government only withholds info when UFOs are from other planets (Option C), then their withholding supports the conclusion. That seems strong.\n\nOption A says they deny requests only if they have something to hide. But having something to hide doesn't necessarily mean it's about UFOs being extraterrestrial. It could be other reasons, like national security or something else. So while it supports the idea that there's a reason to hide, it doesn't specifically tie it to UFOs being extraterrestrial.\n\nTherefore, Option C is stronger because it directly links the withholding of information to the conclusion that UFOs are extraterrestrial.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
