of all areas changes slowly but significantly, sometimes to the advantage of certain flora and fauna. This is a bit more general. It talks about ecological changes over time, but it doesn't specifically tie into the water contamination issue. It might even suggest that changes could be good or bad, but it doesn't directly support the argument that peat harvesting won't contaminate water.\n\nOption D: The activities of other industries in coming years are likely to have adverse effects on the water supply of this country. This seems to introduce another factor that could affect water supply. But the argument is about peat harvesting specifically. If other industries are causing problems, that doesn't necessarily strengthen the case for peat harvesting being safe. It might even distract from the main point.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the strongest support comes from Option B because it establishes that the conditions in Ireland were similar to here, making the comparison more valid. If the original ecologies were the same, then the lack of contamination in Ireland is a stronger indicator that it won't happen here either.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
