      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context talks about people who know health knowledge but lack self-discipline. It gives examples like staying up late to watch TV, smoking and drinking despite knowing it's bad, and not exercising even though they know it's good. The main point seems to be that self-discipline is key. People with self-discipline go to bed early, avoid certain foods, and exercise. If someone adheres to self-discipline, they won't indulge themselves and can maintain their circadian rhythm, leading to a healthy and happy life.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options can be obtained based on the statement. Let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: All people who stick to sports are very disciplined. Hmm, the context says that people with self-discipline do exercise, but does it say that everyone who exercises is disciplined? I don't think so. Maybe someone exercises but isn't disciplined in other areas. So A might not be correct.\n\nOption B: Some people who lack self-discipline do not lack health knowledge. The context says that many people are not lacking in health knowledge but lack self-discipline. So this means that some people who lack self-discipline actually do have health knowledge. That seems to fit. So B could be correct.\n\nOption C: If a person does not adhere to the spirit of self-discipline, he will indulge himself. The context says that if someone adheres to self-discipline, they won't indulge. But does it say that not adhering leads to indulgence? It's a bit of a leap. The context doesn't explicitly state that non-adherence causes indulgence, just that adherence prevents it. So C might not be necessarily true.\n\nOption D: People who maintain their circadian rhythm can live a healthy and happy life. The context says that adhering to self-discipline allows one to maintain circadian rhythm and live hea