      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. \n\nSmithtown University's fundraisers got donations from 80% of the people they contacted. That's a really high success rate, which is unusual for universities. But the argument is that this high success rate doesn't mean they're doing a good job. Instead, it shows they're not putting in enough effort. Why? Because good fundraisers should be targeting people who are less likely to donate, like new donors, to expand the donor base. If they're only contacting people who are likely to donate (like past donors), their success rate would naturally be high, but they're not actually expanding their donor base. So the high rate indicates they're not canvassing enough new people.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would support this argument. So I need to find the option that provides more evidence that the fundraisers aren't canvassing enough new prospects.\n\nLet's look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: This year, the average donation from new donors was larger than from past donors. Hmm, so if new donors gave more on average, does that support the argument? The argument is about the success rate being high because they're targeting past donors, not about the size of donations. So I'm not sure if this directly supports the argument. It might be a red herring because it's talking about the amount, not the number of new donors contacted.\n\nOption B: Most of the donations came from people who had never donated before. Wait, that's interesting. If the majority were new donors, that would mean the fundraisers were successfully getting donations from new people. But the argument says that a high success rate implies they're not canvassing enough new people. So if most donations were from new donors, that would contradict the argument, right? Because it would suggest they are expanding the donor base. So this might actually weaken the argument, not support it.\n\nOption C: Most donations from past donors came without the fundraisers contacting them. Oh, that's a good point. If past donors are giving without being contacted, that means the fundraisers didn't have to do much to get those donations. So the high success rate is because they're contacting people who would have donated anyway. This supports the argument that they're not canvassing enough new people because they're relying on past donors who don't need to be contacted. So this seems to support the argument.\n\nOption D: The success rate with new donors is about the same as other universities. So if they're as successful as others in getting new donors, does that support the argument? The argument is that their high overall success rate is because they're not canvassing enough new people. If their success rate with new donors is average, that doesn't directly support the argument. It might even suggest they're doing okay in that area, which doesn't help the argument that they're not canvassing enough.\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument is that a high success rate (80%) is bad because it means they're only contacting easy targets (past donors) and not expanding. So if most of their donations are from new donors (Option B), that would mean they are expanding, which would contradict the argument. So Option B is not supporting.\n\nOption C says that past donors gave without being contacted. So the fundraisers didn't have to contact them, which means the high success rate is because they're contacting past donors who would have donated anyway. This means they're not canvassing new people, which supports the argument.\n\nSo the correct answer should be Option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
