ause they hear a stream. So this might not be an assumption because the argument doesn't claim that understanding is impossible, just that non-speakers don't understand it.\n\nOption D: People pay less attention to their own language's sounds than to unfamiliar ones. The argument doesn't discuss attention levels. It's more about the perception of words versus a stream. So this might not be an assumption because the argument doesn't rely on how much attention people pay.\n\nSo, going back, the argument is that native speakers think they hear separate words, but it's an illusion because non-speakers hear a stream. The assumption here is that native speakers' perception isn't more accurate than non-speakers. So Option A seems to be the correct assumption because the argument is comparing the two groups and concluding that the native speakers' perception is an illusion, implying their accuracy isn't better.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The argument is that because non-speakers hear a stream, native speakers' perception of separate words is an illusion. So the assumption must be that if non-speakers can't perceive words, then native speakers' ability to do so isn't actually better. So yes, Option A is the assumption because it's saying that native speakers aren't more accurate, which allows the conclusion that their perception is an illusion.\n\nI think I'm confident now that Option A is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe argument hinges on the assumption that native speakers' perception of their language isn't more accurate than non-speakers', which allows the conclusion that their perception of separate words is an illusion.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
