spending more on Internet ads but making more revenue, that could explain it. But I'm not sure if this directly resolves the paradox. It might just mean they're investing more, but the paradox is about the revenue relative to audience size. So maybe C isn't the best fit.\n\nOption D: Internet advertisements allow the firm to more accurately target audiences. Oh, this makes sense. If the Internet ads can target specific audiences more precisely, even though the overall audience is smaller, the conversion rate or the effectiveness might be higher. So even with a smaller audience, each ad might be more effective, leading to higher revenue. That would explain why two-thirds of revenue comes from the Internet despite the smaller audience. So D seems like a good candidate.\n\nLet me think again. The paradox is that a smaller audience on the Internet is generating more revenue. So the key is why that's happening. If the Internet ads are more targeted, they might be reaching the right people who are more likely to buy, so each ad is more valuable. That would make the revenue higher even with fewer people seeing the ads. So D resolves the paradox by explaining that the smaller audience is more valuable because of better targeting.\n\nSo the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
