ment is partially true and partially false, which doesn't fit because the question is about which determination is true. So, perhaps this scenario doesn't support option A.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should consider that if A is true, and E is true, then V must be true, but that would make D also true, which is not allowed. So, the only way A is true without D being true is if E is false, which makes V false as well. But then, option A's second part is false, so perhaps this isn't the right path.\n\nLet me move on to the next option.\n\n**Option B: B is true.**\n\nIf B is true, then \"V and not E\" is true. So, V is true, E is false.\n\nNow, check the other statements to see if they are false.\n\nCheck A: \"If E, then V.\" Since E is false, A's statement is automatically true. But that's a problem because only B should be true. So, A would also be true, which contradicts the condition. Therefore, B cannot be the true statement because it would make A also true.\n\nSo, option B is invalid.\n\n**Option C: C is true.**\n\nIf C is true, then \"E and not V\" is true. So, E is true, V is false.\n\nNow, check the other statements.\n\nCheck A: \"If E, then V.\" Since E is true and V is false, A's statement is false. Good.\n\nCheck B: \"V and not E.\" V is false, so B is false. Good.\n\nCheck D: \"E.\" E is true, so D is true. But that's a problem because only C should be true. So, D is also true, which contradicts the condition. Therefore, C cannot be the true statement.\n\n**Option D: D is true.**\n\nIf D is true, then E is true.\n\nNow, check the other statements.\n\nCheck A: \"If E, then V.\" Since E is true, for A to be false, V must be false. So, V is false.\n\nCheck B: \"V and not E.\" V is false, so B is false. Good.\n\nCheck C: \"E and not V.\" E is true, V is false, so C is true. But that's a problem because only D should be true. So, C is also true, which contradicts the condition. Therefore, D cannot be the true statement.\n\nWait, so none of the options seem to f