      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem. Let me read through it again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nWe have three traffic police officers: A, B, and C. They've done a safety check on the city's passenger cars during the spring festival. Each of them has made a conclusion:\n\n- A says: All the passenger cars are overloaded.\n- B says: There is no overload problem on all the city's passenger buses.\n- C says: Both Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded.\n\nThe question is, if only one of these three conclusions is wrong, which of the options A, B, C, D must be true.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out what each statement means and how they relate to each other. Let's break them down.\n\nA's statement: \"All the passenger cars are overloaded.\" So, according to A, every single passenger car in the city is overloaded. That's a strong statement because it's a universal claim.\n\nB's statement: \"There is no overload problem on all the city's passenger buses.\" Hmm, this is a bit tricky. The wording is a bit confusing. Let me parse it. \"There is no overload problem on all the city's passenger buses.\" So, does B mean that none of the passenger buses are overloaded? Or does it mean that not all passenger buses are overloaded? Wait, the way it's phrased is a bit confusing. Let me think. If B says \"there is no overload problem on all the city's passenger buses,\" that could be interpreted as \"for all passenger buses, there is no overload problem,\" meaning none are overloaded. Alternatively, it could be interpreted as \"it's not the case that all passenger buses are overloaded,\" meaning at least one is not overloaded. But in logic, the way it's phrased is important. The original statement is \"there is no overload problem on all the city's passenger buses.\" So, it's saying that for all buses, there is no overload. So, B is saying that none of the passenger buses are overloaded. So, B's statement is that all passenger buses are no