t what about passenger buses? B is wrong, so at least one passenger bus is overloaded. But C says both Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded. So, that would mean that Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded, which is consistent with B being wrong because B said none were overloaded.\n\nSo, in this scenario, A and C are correct, B is wrong. That seems possible.\n\n3. Now, suppose C is wrong. Then, C's statement is incorrect. C said both Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded. So, if C is wrong, then it's not the case that both are overloaded. That could mean either one is not overloaded or neither is overloaded.\n\nNow, if C is wrong, then A and B must be correct.\n\nA is correct: All passenger cars are overloaded.\n\nB is correct: All passenger buses are not overloaded.\n\nSo, if B is correct, then no passenger buses are overloaded. That includes Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses. So, both Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are not overloaded. But C said both were overloaded, so C is wrong, which is consistent.\n\nSo, in this scenario, A and B are correct, C is wrong. That also seems possible.\n\nSo, we have two possible scenarios:\n\n- Scenario 1: A correct, B wrong, C correct. But wait, earlier I thought that if A is wrong, it leads to a contradiction, so A can't be wrong. So, the only possible scenarios are when either B is wrong or C is wrong.\n\nWait, no, in the first scenario, I considered A being wrong, but that led to a contradiction because B and C can't both be correct. So, A can't be wrong. So, only B or C can be wrong.\n\nSo, the two possible scenarios are:\n\na) B is wrong, so A and C are correct.\n\nb) C is wrong, so A and B are correct.\n\nNow, let's analyze each scenario.\n\nScenario a: B is wrong, A and C correct.\n\nA correct: All passenger cars are overloaded.\n\nC correct: Both Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded.\n\nSo, in this case, passenger cars are all overloaded, and Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded. But B is wrong, so at least one