sts that the decline wasn't due to environmental factors like tree cutting but rather due to external invasions. This would support the experts' view that tree felling isn't the main reason.\n\nNow, I need to decide between options B and D. Both seem to support the experts' opinion, but in different ways. Option B shows that even after trees were gone, the society didn't collapse, implying trees weren't the main issue. Option D points to an external cause (colonization) as the main factor.\n\nThe question is about supporting the experts' opinion that the decline isn't necessarily related to tree felling. Both B and D do that, but D provides a specific alternative cause, which is a strong support. However, the question is about which option, if true, would best support the experts. So I think D is stronger because it introduces a direct cause (colonization) that explains the decline without needing to blame tree cutting.\n\nWait, but the question is about the decline of the civilization, not just the population. Option B shows that even after trees were gone, the agricultural level didn't decline, which directly contradicts the traditional view that tree loss led to agricultural failure. So B is also strong.\n\nI'm a bit torn. Let me think again. The traditional view is that tree cutting led to soil loss and food shortages. If B is true, it shows that even after all trees were cut, agriculture was still fine, so the decline must be due to something else. That directly supports the experts' view. Option D also supports it but by introducing another factor. Both are good, but B is more directly related to the environmental impact of tree cutting.\n\nSo I think the best answer is B because it directly shows that the loss of trees didn't lead to agricultural decline, which is the main point of the traditional view.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
