      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. \n\nMayor Stephens created a special task force to fight corruption in the city government. This task force is now prosecuting a dozen city officials. All of these officials were appointed by Mayor Bixby, who was Stephens' predecessor and a political rival. The editorial's argument is that these officials are being prosecuted because of their political affiliations, not necessarily because they're corrupt.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would most weaken this argument. So I need to find the option that undermines the idea that the prosecutions are politically motivated.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Complaints of corruption have decreased since the task force started. Hmm, if corruption complaints went down, that might suggest the task force is effective. But does that weaken the argument that they're targeting based on politics? Maybe, because if they're actually reducing corruption, it's less likely to be just about politics. But I'm not sure if this is the strongest option.\n\nOption B: Almost all city officials are appointees of Mayor Bixby. Wait, if most officials are from Bixby, then prosecuting a dozen of them might just be because they're the majority, not necessarily because of politics. So if the task force is going after the majority, it might not be targeting based on politics but rather because they're the ones in office. This could weaken the argument because it shows that the task force isn't specifically targeting Bixby's appointees; they're just the ones who are there.\n\nOption C: Bixby didn't oppose the task force. So Bixby didn't speak out against it. I'm not sure how this affects the argument. Maybe if Bixby didn't oppose, it doesn't necessarily mean the task force isn't politically motivated. It could still be about politics regardless of Bixby's stance.\n\nOption D: All task force members had other jobs before. This might suggest that they're not specialized in anticorruption, but does that weaken the argument? Maybe it implies they're not effective, but I'm not sure it directly addresses the political motivation.\n\nSo, thinking again, the editorial's argument is that the task force is targeting Bixby's appointees because of politics. To weaken this, we need to show that the targeting isn't about politics but about something else, like the fact that most officials are from Bixby.\n\nOption B says that almost all officials are Bixby's appointees. So if the task force is prosecuting a dozen, and most officials are Bixby's, then it's not specifically targeting them because of politics; it's just that they're the ones in the system. That would weaken the argument because it shows the prosecution is more about the officials' positions rather than their political affiliations.\n\nOption A is about effectiveness, which might support the task force's legitimacy but doesn't directly address the political motive. Option D is about the task force's background, which might question their expertise but not the motive. Option C is about Bixby's reaction, which doesn't directly relate to the motive.\n\nSo I think the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
