      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. The context says that some health officials are worried about the increase in deaths from alcohol-related conditions. They think this is because more people are becoming alcoholics. But the argument presented here is that the increase in reported deaths isn't necessarily because there's more alcoholism, but rather because attitudes toward alcoholism have changed. Before, alcoholism was seen as a moral failing, but now it's viewed as a disease. So, because of this shift, doctors are more likely to identify deaths as alcohol-related, which makes the reported numbers go up.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would support this argument the most. So I need to find the option that strengthens the idea that the increase in reported deaths is due to doctors now recognizing alcoholism as a disease, not because there's actually more alcoholism.\n\nLet me look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: The frequent use of alcohol by young people is being reported as occurring at increasingly early ages. Hmm, this seems to talk about the age of onset, but I'm not sure how that directly supports the argument. The argument is about doctors identifying deaths as alcohol-related because of changed attitudes, not about when people start drinking. So maybe this isn't the best support.\n\nOption B: Even though alcoholism is considered a disease, most doctors recommend psychological counseling and support groups as the best treatment. This is about treatment methods. The argument is about identification, not treatment. So while it's related, it doesn't directly support the idea that more deaths are being reported because doctors are now recognizing alcoholism as a disease. It's more about what doctors do after identifying it, not the identification itself.\n\nOption C: In some places and times, susceptibility to any kind of disease has been viewed as a moral failing. This seems to be a general statement about how diseases were viewed in the past. The argument is about a shift in attitude specifically towards alcoholism. While it's somewhat related, it's not directly supporting the idea that more deaths are being reported because of this shift. It's more of a background point rather than a direct support.\n\nOption D: More physicians now than in the past are trained to recognize the physical effects of alcoholism. Oh, this seems relevant. If more doctors are trained to recognize the physical effects, they would be better at identifying when a death is alcohol-related. This directly supports the argument that the increase in reported deaths is because doctors are now more likely to identify these cases, not because there's an actual increase in alcoholism. So this option strengthens the argument by providing a reason why doctors are better at identifying these deaths now.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the argument hinges on the idea that doctors are now more likely to attribute deaths to alcoholism because of changed attitudes. Option D provides a specific reason why that would be the case\u2014more training in recognizing the effects. Therefore, D is the best support.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
