etic, does that mean he must be from Zhenyuan? Not necessarily. Because there might be other places where people are ascetics. The context doesn't say that only Zhenyuan people are ascetics, just that all Zhenyuan people are. So, Guo Shu could be from Zhenyuan, but he could also be from somewhere else. Therefore, we can't conclude that he is not from Zhenyuan. So, B might not be correct either.\n\nOption C: Guo Shu is from Anxi. But wait, all Anxi people are vegetarians. And since Guo Shu is an ascetic, and ascetics and vegetarians don't mix, Guo Shu cannot be a vegetarian. Therefore, he cannot be from Anxi because that would make him a vegetarian, which contradicts his status as an ascetic. So, C is incorrect.\n\nOption D: Guo Shu is not from Anxi. This makes sense because if he were from Anxi, he'd have to be a vegetarian, but he's an ascetic, and they don't mix. So, D seems correct.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says that Anxi people are all vegetarians, and Zhenyuan people are all ascetics. Ascetics and vegetarians don't mix, meaning no overlap. Guo Shu is an ascetic, so he can't be a vegetarian. Therefore, he can't be from Anxi because that would make him a vegetarian. So, D is correct.\n\nBut what about Zhenyuan? Since all Zhenyuan people are ascetics, Guo Shu could be from Zhenyuan, but he doesn't have to be. The context doesn't say that only Zhenyuan people are ascetics. So, we can't infer that he is from Zhenyuan (A is not necessarily true), nor can we infer that he's not from Zhenyuan (B is not necessarily true). So, the only definite inference is that he's not from Anxi, which is option D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
