hat the premises don't lead to the conclusion, but rather that the sample isn't big enough to support the conclusion.\n</think>\n\nGamba's argument focuses on the small sample size of the vote, which he uses to cast doubt on the conclusion that the entire city opposes the new water system. He emphasizes that 15 opposing votes represent a tiny fraction of the population, making the sample unreliable. This aligns with option B, which involves questioning the conclusion due to an insufficient statistical sample.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
