      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand what's being asked.\n\nThe context says that Innovair is planning to reduce ground collisions by giving their ground crews additional training. This training will cost $500,000 annually. They mention that this training will cut the number of ground collisions by about half at best. However, the drop in repair costs is expected to be much greater. The question is asking which option most logically completes the argument, given that in a typical year, there are 35 collisions with a repair cost of $1,000,000.\n\nSo, the argument is that even though the number of collisions is only reduced by half, the repair costs will drop much more. The blank is explaining why that is the case. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break it down. The key point is that the reduction in repair costs is greater than the reduction in collision frequency. So, if collisions are reduced by half, but repair costs drop by more than half, that suggests that the remaining collisions are less costly. Therefore, the training must be targeting the most expensive collisions.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Most ground collisions have been caused by the least experienced ground-crew members. Hmm, this might explain why training would help, but does it explain why repair costs drop more? Maybe, but not directly. If the least experienced cause most collisions, training them would reduce collisions, but does that necessarily mean the remaining collisions are less costly? Not necessarily. It could be that the experienced ones cause more costly collisions, but this option doesn't specify that.\n\nB) A ground collision typically occurs when there are no passengers on the airplane. I'm not sure how this relates. If there are no passengers, maybe the damage is less because there's no risk to people, but the repair costs might still be the same. Or maybe without passengers, the plane is lighter, so damage is less. But this seems a bit of a stretch. The argument is about the training's effect, not the circumstances of the collisions.\n\nC) The additional training will focus on helping ground crews avoid those kinds of ground collisions that cause the most costly damage. This seems directly relevant. If the training targets the most costly collisions, then even if the number of collisions is only halved, the ones that remain are the less costly ones. So the total repair costs would drop more than proportionally. For example, if half the collisions are the most expensive ones, reducing those by half would save a lot more money than just reducing the total number.\n\nD) The $500,000 cost figure includes the wages during training. This is about the cost of training, not about why repair costs would drop more. So this doesn't explain the relationship between the reduction in collisions and the repair costs.\n\nSo, putting it together, the argument is that the training reduces collisions by half, but repair costs drop more because the training focuses on the most expensive collisions. Therefore, the correct answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
