      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says: \"Any paper involving national military, political or economic secrets is not a public paper.\" So, if a paper has any of these secrets, it can't be public. That means if a paper is about military, political, or economic secrets, it's not public. So, in logical terms, I can represent this as: If a paper involves secrets (let's call that S), then it is not public (let's call that P). So, S \u2192 \u00acP.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options must be true based on this assertion. Let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Papers that do not involve national military, political or economic secrets cannot be made public.\" Hmm, so this is saying that if a paper doesn't involve S, then it can't be public. Wait, that doesn't seem right. The original statement only tells us about papers that do involve S. It doesn't say anything about papers that don't involve S. So, the original statement doesn't provide information about whether non-S papers can be public or not. So, Option A might not necessarily be true.\n\nOption B: \"All unpublic papers are about the national military, political or economic secrets.\" So, this is saying that if a paper is not public (\u00acP), then it must involve S. But the original statement is S \u2192 \u00acP, which is not the same as \u00acP \u2192 S. In logic, the contrapositive of S \u2192 \u00acP is P \u2192 \u00acS, which is different. So, just because a paper isn't public doesn't mean it must involve S. There could be other reasons why a paper isn't public. So, Option B isn't necessarily true.\n\nOption C: \"Some papers involving national military, political or economic secrets can be made public.\" Wait, the original statement says that any paper involving S is not public. So, if a paper involves S, it can't be public. Therefore, it's impossible for any such paper to be public. So, Option C is saying the opposite of what the original statement says. Therefore, Option C must be false.\n\nOption D: \"None of the public papers are involved in national military, political or economic secrets.\" Let's break this down. If a paper is public (P), then it doesn't involve S. Because the original statement says S \u2192 \u00acP, which is equivalent to P \u2192 \u00acS. So, if a paper is public, it can't involve S. Therefore, none of the public papers involve S. So, Option D is correct.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The original statement is S \u2192 \u00acP. The contrapositive is P \u2192 \u00acS, which is exactly what Option D is saying. So, yes, Option D must be true.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
