      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option strengthens the climatologist's argument. Let me start by understanding the argument first.\n\nThe climatologist says that because of global warming, winter temperatures in the Rocky Mountains will increase. This will cause more precipitation to fall as rain instead of snow. As a result, the snowpack will melt more rapidly and earlier in the season. This leads to two main consequences: greater spring flooding and less water stored for summer needs.\n\nSo the argument is: warmer winters \u2192 more rain, less snow \u2192 snow melts faster and earlier \u2192 more spring floods and less water in summer.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option, if true, would most strengthen this argument. I need to look at each option and see how it supports the argument.\n\nOption A: Global warming will cause a substantial increase in annual precipitation in the Rockies. Hmm, the original argument talks about the type of precipitation (more rain, less snow) but not necessarily the amount. If there's more precipitation overall, that could mean more water, but does it directly support the argument? Maybe, but I'm not sure. The argument is about the timing and form of precipitation, not the total amount. So this might not be the strongest support.\n\nOption B: In regions with mild winters, there's more spring flooding and less storable water than in colder regions. This seems relevant because the argument is about milder winters leading to more spring flooding. If this is true, it provides a general pattern that supports the climatologist's conclusion. So this could be a good candidate.\n\nOption C: Larger snowpacks cause more spring flooding. Wait, the argument is about the snowpack melting more rapidly, not necessarily being larger. If the snowpack is larger, it might lead to more flooding, but the argument is about the rate of melting. So this might not directly strengthen the argument. It's more about the size