      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best supports the expert's prediction that traditional bank branches will disappear within 20 years. Let me start by understanding the context given.\n\nThe context says that community banks in big cities are closing down frequently, and new types of banks like unmanned, 5G, and smart banks are emerging. These new banks are moving away from traditional setups with cold glass counters and metal benches. Instead, they're focusing less on transaction processing and more on customized services, product experiences, and social interactions. Experts predict that traditional branches will vanish in 20 years.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options, if true, best supports this prediction. So I need to find the option that provides the strongest evidence for why traditional branches are likely to disappear.\n\nLet's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Customers need to enter the door, take a number, wait, and even simple transactions take a long time. This suggests that traditional branches are inconvenient. If this is true, it supports the idea that people might prefer alternatives, which could lead to the decline of traditional branches. So this could be a supporting factor.\n\nOption B: The introduction of AI and other tech has changed people's impressions of bank branches. This seems positive, implying that technology is making branches better or more appealing. But the expert's view is that branches will disappear, so if tech is improving branches, this might not support the prediction. It could even go against it because better tech might make branches more relevant. So I'm not sure if this supports the expert's view.\n\nOption C: Complex businesses like account opening or cancellation require face-to-face interaction and manual audits. This suggests that some services still need to be done in person. If that's the case, it might mean that traditional branches are still necessary for these tasks, which could counter the expert's prediction. So this might not support the expert's view.\n\nOption D: Online and mobile banking are emerging, and bank outlets are becoming less of the main service channel. This directly points to the decline of traditional branches as the primary service method. If online and mobile are taking over, then traditional branches would become less important, supporting the expert's prediction.\n\nNow, comparing the options. Option A talks about inconvenience, which could lead people to seek alternatives. Option D talks about the shift in service channels, which directly affects the relevance of traditional branches. Option D seems more directly related to the expert's prediction because it's about the main service channels changing, which would cause branches to close.\n\nSo, the best support would be Option D because it shows that the main service is moving away from physical branches, which aligns with the expert's view that traditional branches will disappear.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
