      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. \n\nThe context says that parents are paying more attention to their children's reading. In 2018, 70.3% of families in a certain city had the habit of parents reading with their children. That's an increase of 1.8 percentage points compared to 2017. Also, the amount of books read by parents and children went up, and the length of the books they read also increased. But here's the thing: despite all that, the number of books read per child in 2018 was 4.72, which is down by 0.6 percentage points from 2017. So, even though more families are reading together and the books are longer, the average number of books per child actually decreased. That seems a bit contradictory, right? So the question is asking which option best explains this phenomenon.\n\nLet me look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: In recent years, children have a heavy burden of schoolwork and many people want to read extracurricular reading but do not have enough time. Hmm, that makes sense because if kids are busy with schoolwork, they might not have time to read more books. But wait, the context says that the amount of books read by parents and children increased. So if they're reading more, but the average per child is down, maybe this isn't the best explanation. Or maybe the increase in reading time is offset by the heavy schoolwork, but I'm not sure yet.\n\nOption B: Most parents born in the 1980s and 1990s are highly educated and value reading for their children. That seems positive. If more educated parents are reading with their kids, that should lead to more books being read, not fewer. So this might not explain the decrease in the average number of books per child. It actually supports the increase, so maybe this isn't the answer.\n\nOption C: Many children are used to listening to books in parent-child reading, which is not counted in 2018. Oh, that's interesting. So if the children are listening to books (like audiobooks or someone reading to them), but that's not being counted as reading, then the actual number of books they've read (in terms of physical books they've read themselves) might be lower. So even though they're engaging with more content, it's not being counted as individual books read by the child. That could explain why the average is down. Because the context mentions that the amount of books read increased, but maybe that's referring to the total content, not the number of physical books each child reads on their own.\n\nOption D: Electronic reading by parents on mobile phones and computers has not been taken into account in children's books. So if parents are reading electronically, maybe that's not being counted towards the children's reading. But the context says that the amount of books read by parents and children increased. So if electronic reading is part of that, then it's already counted. Unless the children's reading isn't including electronic books, but the context doesn't specify. So I'm not sure if this is the best explanation.\n\nLet me think again. The key point is that while more families are reading together, the average number of books per child is down. So the increase in reading habits and book length might not translate to more books read per child. \n\nOption C suggests that listening to books isn't counted. So if children are listening to more books (which counts as reading for the family), but they're not actually reading the books themselves, then the number of books each child reads individually might go down. That makes sense because the family's total reading time and content might be up, but the child's personal reading count is down because they're just listening instead of reading.\n\nOption A talks about schoolwork, but if the amount of books read by parents and children increased, that might mean they're making time despite schoolwork. So maybe A isn't the best fit.\n\nSo I think the best answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
