      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand what's being asked.\n\nThe context is about whether we should charge people for rescue operations when they go on intentional adventures that require public resources. There are two sides: the positive side thinks we should charge, and the negative side thinks we shouldn't.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options, if true, cannot support the positive side's viewpoint. So, I need to find the option that doesn't help the argument for charging.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"A rational citizen should be responsible for his own actions; otherwise, it will be unfair to other taxpayers.\" This seems to support the positive side because it's saying that if someone takes risks, they should be responsible, and charging them would be fair to others who pay taxes. So A supports the positive side.\n\nOption B: \"The establishment of a professional rescue system requires a certain amount of financial support.\" This is talking about the need for funds to have a rescue system. If we charge for rescues, it could help fund the system. So B also supports the positive side.\n\nOption C: \"It is the duty of government departments to rescue people in distress. Simple charges can not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility.\" Hmm, this is a bit tricky. The first part says it's the government's duty to rescue, which might argue against charging because it's their responsibility. The second part says that charging won't help build social responsibility. So this seems to go against the positive side's argument. It suggests that charging isn't effective in making people more responsible, which would mean we shouldn't charge. So C doesn't support the positive side.\n\nOption D: \"Tourist explorers who illegally enter unsafe areas and waste social resources and taxpayers' money should bear the corresponding expenses.\" This directly supports charging because it says those who misuse resources should pay. So D is on the positive side.\n\nSo, the question is asking which option cannot support the positive side. From my analysis, option C is the one that doesn't support the positive side because it argues against charging, saying it won't help with social responsibility and that rescue is the government's duty.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
