      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem about four people applying for a Chinese traditional culture construction project. The project can only have one moderator, and no more than two team members, including the moderator. Each of the four applicants made a statement, and all of them are true. I need to figure out which combination of team members is impossible based on these statements.\n\nFirst, let me list out the four applicants: Yan Zi, Zeng Yin, Meng Shen, and Xun Chen. The moderator is one of them, and the team can have at most two people, so the moderator plus one member.\n\nEach person's statement:\n\n1. Yan Zi says: If I become the host, I will invite Zeng Yin or Xun Chen as members.\n2. Zeng Yin says: If I become the host, I will invite Yan Zi or Meng Shen as members.\n3. Xun Chen says: I can be the host only if Yan Zi becomes a member.\n4. Meng Shen says: Only if Xun Chen or Yan Zi become members can I become the host.\n\nSo, all four statements are true. I need to consider each possible moderator and see what the implications are for the team members, then check which of the options A-D is impossible.\n\nLet me go through each possible moderator one by one.\n\n1. Moderator is Yan Zi:\n   - According to Yan Zi's statement, if he is the host, he will invite Zeng Yin or Xun Chen. So the team would be Yan Zi + either Zeng Yin or Xun Chen. So possible teams: {Yan Zi, Zeng Yin} or {Yan Zi, Xun Chen}.\n   - Now, check the other statements to see if they hold.\n   - Zeng Yin's statement: If Zeng Yin were the host, he would invite Yan Zi or Meng Shen. But since Yan Zi is the host, Zeng Yin's statement doesn't affect this scenario.\n   - Xun Chen's statement: He can only be the host if Yan Zi is a member. Since Yan Zi is the host, Xun Chen's statement is satisfied if he is a member. But in this case, Xun Chen might be a member or not, depending on Yan Zi's choice.\n   - Meng Shen's statement: He can only be the host if Xun Chen or