eir own schedules.\" The context doesn't talk about accepting lower wages. It's about setting a target and working until they reach it, which gives them some schedule flexibility. But the fact that they work fewer hours on busy days doesn't directly address whether they accept lower wages for that freedom. So I'm not sure about this one.\n\nWait, let me think again. The taxi drivers set a daily income target, so they stop when they reach it. On busy days, they reach the target faster, so they work fewer hours. On slow days, they have to work longer to meet the same target. So their effective hourly wage is higher on busy days because they make more money in less time. \n\nNow, looking at option C: It says people work longer when their effective hourly wage is high. But in reality, they work shorter hours when the hourly wage is high. So the facts contradict option C. Therefore, the strongest evidence is against option C.\n\nI think that's the right approach. The key is that higher hourly wage leads to fewer hours worked, which is the opposite of what option C states.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
