f deception. The argument doesn't discuss whether it's justified or not, just that it's effective. So this might not be an assumption but rather a conclusion or a separate point.\n\nOption D: Most of the techniques that help people quit smoking can also help people break other habits. The context does mention that similar strategies can be used for other habits, so this seems like a premise rather than an assumption. The argument is built on that premise, so it's not an assumption but a given.\n\nSo, the argument depends on the idea that people can't easily deceive themselves, which is option B. Without this assumption, the argument wouldn't hold because if people could easily deceive themselves, they wouldn't need a third party to provide the exaggerated warnings.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The argument says that individuals cannot easily adopt these strategies unless a third party provides the warning. So the assumption is that individuals cannot easily deceive themselves, which is option B. That makes sense because if people could deceive themselves, they wouldn't need a doctor or third party.\n\nOption A is more about the effectiveness of exaggeration, which is presented as a fact in the context, so it's not an assumption but a premise. The argument doesn't assume that exaggeration works; it states that it does. So the assumption is about the difficulty of self-deception, which is option B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
