on.\" The context talks about human error and plumbing flaws, not about the design compensating for material failures. So I don't think the context supports this option. It doesn't mention anything about design or materials, so C seems unrelated.\n\nOption D: \"If nuclear power plants continue in operation, a serious accident at such a plant is not improbable.\" The context says that these everyday events (like human error and plumbing issues) cannot be thought unlikely to occur over the long run. So if plants keep operating, it's not improbable that such accidents will happen. That aligns with what D is saying. So D seems to be supported by the context.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says that initial causes have been human error or plumbing flaws, and these are not unlikely over time. So if plants keep running, it's likely that such accidents will happen again. So D is saying that a serious accident is not improbable, which matches the context.\n\nSo the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe context states that serious accidents at nuclear power plants have been caused by human error or plumbing issues, not advanced technology. It also mentions that such everyday events are not unlikely over time. Option D aligns with this by stating that continued operation makes a serious accident not improbable. \n\nThe correct option is: D",
