complicate things. Let's see.\n\nWait, no, the constraint is that there's exactly one day between A and D, so it could be A before D or D before A. But since B must be before D, if D is before A, then B must be before D, which is before A. So let's consider both possibilities.\n\nLet me try to list possible A and D positions:\n\nCase 1: A is before D.\n\n- A on Monday, D on Wednesday.\n\n- A on Tuesday, D on Thursday.\n\n- A on Wednesday, D on Friday.\n\nCase 2: D is before A.\n\n- D on Monday, A on Wednesday.\n\n- D on Tuesday, A on Thursday.\n\n- D on Wednesday, A on Friday.\n\nBut wait, if D is on Monday, then B must be before D, which would mean B is on Monday as well, but each person is on a unique day. So D can't be on Monday because B has to be before D, and B can't be on the same day as D. So D can't be on Monday. Similarly, if D is on Tuesday, then B must be on Monday or Tuesday, but again, B can't be on the same day as D. So B would have to be on Monday if D is on Tuesday.\n\nWait, let's think about this. If D is on Tuesday, then B must be on Monday because B has to be before D. That's possible. So let's consider both cases.\n\nLet me try to map out possible scenarios.\n\nFirst, let's consider Case 1: A is before D.\n\nSubcase 1a: A on Monday, D on Wednesday.\n\nThen, B must be before D, so B can be on Monday, Tuesday, or Wednesday. But A is on Monday, so B can't be on Monday. So B can be on Tuesday or Wednesday. But D is on Wednesday, so B can't be on Wednesday. So B must be on Tuesday.\n\nSo in this subcase:\n\nA: Monday\n\nB: Tuesday\n\nD: Wednesday\n\nNow, we have C and E left for Thursday and Friday.\n\nBut C cannot be on Tuesday, which is already satisfied because C is on Thursday or Friday.\n\nSo possible assignments:\n\nC: Thursday\n\nE: Friday\n\nOr\n\nE: Thursday\n\nC: Friday\n\nBut we also have to ensure that B is before D and E. Since B is on Tuesday, D is on Wednesday, and E is on Thursday or Friday, which are after Tuesday. So that's okay.\n\