      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out what this paragraph is trying to illustrate. Let me read through it again and break it down.\n\nThe first part says that people who silently read TV news and advertising program manuscripts remember more information than those who watch the programs. That suggests that reading might be more effective for retaining information compared to watching TV. \n\nThen it mentions that if you despise the value principle of a magazine, you can still read it with interest. But with TV programs, if you don't agree with the values, you just change the channel quickly. So, with TV, people are less likely to engage with content they disagree with because it's easier to switch. This might mean that TV viewers don't spend time reflecting on conflicting views, leading to fewer doubts and less critical thinking.\n\nThe paragraph also talks about comparing written records being easier to find inconsistencies and distinguish the difference in authority between information. But comparing video reports is more troublesome. So, when given two conflicting TV reports, the audience either relies on intuition or their pre-existing beliefs. This implies that TV might not encourage people to think critically or analyze information as effectively as reading does.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Reading text is more helpful for thinking than watching TV. This seems to align with the points about remembering more, being able to compare written records, and the difficulty in analyzing video reports.\n\nB) The way of information reception affects people's behavior. While the paragraph does discuss how reading vs. watching affects behavior (like changing channels), the main focus seems to be on the impact on thinking and analysis rather than general behavior.\n\nC) Television can make people form wrong values. The paragraph doesn't directly talk about forming values, but more about how TV affects the ability to critically assess information.\n\nD) People who like reading are calmer than those who like watching TV. There's no mention of personality traits like being calm in the paragraph.\n\nSo, the main idea is that reading text helps more with thinking and analysis compared to watching TV. Therefore, the correct answer should be A.\n</think>\n\nThe paragraph emphasizes the superiority of reading text over watching TV in terms of information retention, critical thinking, and analysis. It highlights how reading allows for deeper engagement and reflection, whereas watching TV often leads to quick disengagement and reliance on intuition or prior beliefs. This supports the idea that reading is more beneficial for thinking.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
