      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. \n\nThe context says that when a major political scandal happens before an election, if voters blame the scandal equally on all parties, then almost all incumbents (regardless of their party) get re-elected. But if voters blame the scandal on just one party, then incumbents from that party are likely to lose to challengers from other parties. Also, it's noted that the proportion of incumbents seeking reelection is high and doesn't change much from election to election.\n\nThe question is asking which principle best explains the contrast in voters' reactions. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break this down. So, the key points are:\n\n1. When scandal is blamed equally on all parties: incumbents from all parties are re-elected.\n2. When scandal is blamed on one party: incumbents from that party lose.\n3. Incumbents' reelection rate is high and constant.\n\nI need to find a principle that explains why voters react differently in these two scenarios.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Major scandals can be blamed on incumbents, but whether they're voted out depends on their challengers. Hmm, the context doesn't mention anything about the challengers' quality or who they are. It's more about the blame distribution. So maybe A isn't the best fit.\n\nOption B: When a scandal is blamed on all parties, that judgment is more accurate than blaming one party. Wait, the context doesn't talk about the accuracy of the blame. It's more about the outcome of the election based on who is blamed. So B might not be the right principle.\n\nOption C: When scandals are more the responsibility of parties than individual incumbents, the responsible party must be penalized when possible. That sounds relevant. Because in the first case, when all parties are blamed, maybe the voters don't penalize any party because it's too broad. But when only one party is blamed, they can target that party and penalize it by not re-electing their incumbents. So this seems to fit.\n\nOption D: Incumbents who are rightly blamed shouldn't seek reelection, but if they do, they shouldn't be returned. The context says that incumbents do seek reelection regardless, and whether they get re-elected depends on who's blamed. So D doesn't quite fit because it's more about the incumbents' own decision, not the voters' reaction based on blame distribution.\n\nSo, putting it together, the principle in C seems to explain the contrast. When the blame is spread across all parties, voters can't effectively penalize any single party, so incumbents are re-elected. But when only one party is blamed, voters can target that party, leading to incumbents losing.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
