s.\n\nOption A: G is assigned to sales.\n\nIf G is in sales, then sales would have H, Y, G. That's three people. Then public relations would have W. Production has F, X, I. That works because all departments are filled correctly.\n\nOption B: H is assigned to production.\n\nWait, earlier I thought H can't be in production because that would require Y to be in production as well, making four people in production. So H can't be in production because that would exceed the department's capacity. So option B says H is in production, which would require Y to be there too, making four people, which is impossible. So option B cannot be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If H is in production, Y must be in production as well. So production would have F, X, H, Y. That's four people, but production can only have three. So that's impossible. Therefore, H cannot be in production. So option B cannot be true.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which statement cannot be true if X and F are in the same department. So if X and F are in production, then H can't be in production because that would require Y to be there too, making four people. So option B is impossible.\n\nBut let me check the other options to make sure.\n\nOption C: I was assigned to sales.\n\nIn the scenario where production is F, X, I, then I is in production. But if I is in sales, then production would have F, X, and someone else. Let's see: if I is in sales, then production would have F, X, and maybe W. So production: F, X, W. Then sales would have H, Y, I. That works because H and Y are together, and sales has three. Public relations would have G. So option C is possible.\n\nOption D: W was assigned to public relations.\n\nIn the scenario where production is F, X, I, then public relations would have W. So that's possible. Alternatively, if production is F, X, W, then public relations would have G. So W can be in public relations if I is in production. So option D is possible.\n\nSo the only option that cannot be true is option B, because H can't be in production without Y, which would exceed the department's capacity.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
