as naive.\n\nI think I'm overcomplicating this. The key point is that despite their naive understanding, their fundamental questions and discussions are a source of inspiration for contemporary scientists. So the purpose is to highlight that their approach is enlightening, which would make A the correct answer because it's about their understanding of the nature of science being enlightening, even if their actual understanding was naive. Alternatively, maybe D is correct because it's about their thoughts providing assistance.\n\nWait, the text doesn't mention their thoughts providing assistance, but their questions and discussions being a source of inspiration. So perhaps the purpose is to say that their approach is enlightening, which would make A correct because it's about their understanding of the nature of science, even if their understanding was naive.\n\nI think I'm stuck between A and D. Let me try to see which option better fits the context.\n\nThe text says that the sages' cognition was naive, but they asked fundamental questions. The purpose is to say that their approach is a source of inspiration for scientists to break free from conventional thinking. So the purpose is to highlight that their method of questioning is enlightening, which would align with A, but A says their understanding is enlightening. Since their understanding was naive, maybe A isn't correct.\n\nAlternatively, the purpose is to say that their questions and discussions (which are part of their approach) are a source of inspiration, which would make the correct answer A, but I'm not sure.\n\nWait, the question is asking for the purpose of the words. The words are trying to convey that even though the sages didn't know much about science, their way of thinking and questioning is valuable for scientists today. So the purpose is to say that their approach is enlightening, which would make A correct because it's about their understanding of the nature of science being enlightening, even if