messages are still effective. That would mean the swindlers are smart because they're using effective methods, which would refute the conclusion that they're stupid. So C could be a strong refutation.\n\nOption D: Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place. This suggests that the messages are designed to target those who are more likely to fall for them, effectively weeding out the smart ones. So the shoddy messages are intentional to filter out the gullible. This would mean the swindlers are actually strategic and professional, which directly refutes the conclusion that they're stupid and unprofessional.\n\nNow, I need to determine which option is the strongest refutation. Let's analyze each.\n\nOption A: It says swindlers must be smart to deceive. If true, it contradicts the conclusion that they're stupid. But it's a bit of a general statement. It doesn't directly explain why they use shoddy messages. So it's a possible refutation but maybe not the strongest.\n\nOption B: It explains that swindlers avoid police attention, so they use simple messages. This implies they're smart because they're avoiding detection. It provides a reason why they don't use more complex messages, which supports their professionalism. So B is a good refutation.\n\nOption C: It states that if the messages were useless, they'd stop. Since they keep using them, the messages must be effective. This suggests the swindlers are smart because they're using effective tactics. So C is another strong refutation.\n\nOption D: It directly states that the messages are used to filter out smart people, meaning the swindlers are targeting the gullible. This shows that the shoddy messages are a strategic choice, indicating they're professional and smart. This directly refutes the conclusion by providing a reason why they use such messages.\n\nComparing all options, D seems to be the strongest because it directly addresses