its instead. If they could accept benefits, the salary might not be increased, so the programs wouldn't be cut. So, D is an assumption because it's necessary for the salary to be increased.\n\nWait, but the conclusion is that programs will be cut, which requires that the salary is increased. So, the argument must assume that the salary is increased. Therefore, the assumption is that the school board will increase the salary. Now, which option corresponds to that.\n\nOption B is about the president's authority. If the president can't offer the increase, the salary won't be increased, leading to a strike, not program cuts. So, B is an assumption.\n\nOption C is that the faculty won't strike, which is the same as the salary being increased. So, C is also an assumption.\n\nOption D is that the faculty won't accept benefits, so the salary must be increased. So, D is an assumption.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which one is assumed for the conclusion to be properly drawn. So, the conclusion is that programs will be cut, which requires that the salary is increased. Therefore, the argument must assume that the salary is increased. So, the assumption is that the school board will increase the salary.\n\nNow, looking at the options, which one is necessary for the salary to be increased.\n\nOption B: The president has the authority to offer the increase. If the president doesn't have the authority, the salary might not be increased, so the conclusion wouldn't follow. So, B is an assumption.\n\nOption C: The faculty won't strike. If they don't strike, that means the salary was increased. So, C is also an assumption.\n\nOption D: The faculty won't accept benefits. If they could accept benefits, the salary might not be increased, so the conclusion wouldn't follow. So, D is an assumption.\n\nWait, but the argument doesn't mention benefits, so the assumption is that the only way to prevent the strike is through a salary increase. Therefore, the argument assumes that the facul