sion unless we assume that S is true.\n\nSo, the argument is assuming that S (salary increase) is true. Therefore, the assumption is that the school board will increase the salary.\n\nNow, which option corresponds to that assumption.\n\nOption B: The president has the authority to offer the increase. If the president can't offer it, S might not happen, so the conclusion wouldn't follow. So, B is an assumption.\n\nOption C: The faculty won't strike. If they don't strike, that means S happened, so C is an assumption.\n\nOption D: The faculty won't accept benefits. If they could accept benefits, S might not happen, so D is an assumption.\n\nBut the question is asking which one is assumed for the conclusion to be properly drawn. So, the conclusion is that programs are cut, which requires that S is true. Therefore, the argument must assume that S is true.\n\nSo, the assumption is that the school board will increase the salary. Now, which option ensures that S is true.\n\nOption B: The president has the authority to offer the increase. If the president can't, S might not happen. So, B is an assumption.\n\nOption C: The faculty won't strike. If they don't strike, S must have happened. So, C is an assumption.\n\nOption D: The faculty won't accept benefits. If they could, S might not happen. So, D is an assumption.\n\nWait, but the argument doesn't mention benefits, so the assumption is that the only way to prevent the strike is through a salary increase. Therefore, the argument assumes that the faculty won't accept alternatives, making D an assumption.\n\nBut also, the argument assumes that the school board can actually increase the salary, which requires that the president has the authority, making B an assumption.\n\nI think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me try to see which option is necessary for the conclusion.\n\nThe conclusion is that programs are cut, which requires that the salary was increased. So, the argument must assume that the salary was increased. Now, the o