the weeks are fixed, so each date would fall on the same day of the week every year. So, for example, if Christmas is on a Sunday one year, it would always be on a Sunday. But if the proposal removes December 31 and the leap day, does that affect the alignment? Let me think. If the year starts on Sunday, then each date would have a fixed day of the week. So, if a holiday is on a Monday, it would always be on a Monday. So, the three-day breaks would still occur as usual because the days of the week are fixed. So, maybe this group wouldn't have conflicts either.\n\nWait, but let me reconsider. The proposal removes December 31 and the leap day from the weekly cycle. So, does that mean that the last week of the year is only six days? Because December 31 is removed. So, the last week would end on December 30, which is a Saturday, and then December 31 is not part of any week. So, the next year starts on Sunday, January 1. So, for someone who has a birthday on December 31, that day is not part of any week, so it's like an extra day. But how does that affect scheduling? Maybe if someone's birthday is on December 31, they might not get a day off because it's not part of the weekly cycle. But the question is about continued scheduling conflicts. So, which group would still have issues.\n\nWait, let's think about the current system. Because the weeks don't align perfectly, anniversaries shift days each year. The proposal aims to fix that by making each year start on Sunday, so each date falls on the same day of the week every year. But the problem is that the last day of the year is removed from the weekly cycle, so December 31 is not part of any week. So, if someone's birthday is on December 31, that day isn't part of any week, so it's not a Monday, Tuesday, etc. So, their birthday would fall on a day that's outside the weekly structure. That might cause issues because, for example, if their birthday is on December 31, which is not part of any week, they might not get a day off or it might not be recognized as a weekend day. So, their scheduling could be affected because their birthday isn't aligned with the weekly cycle.\n\nSimilarly, for someone whose birthday is on December 30, that day is still part of the week, so it would fall on the same day each year. So, maybe only those with birthdays on December 31 would have issues.\n\nLooking back at the options, option B is about people with birthdays or anniversaries on December 30 or 31. So, if December 31 is removed, their birthday on that day wouldn't be part of any week, causing scheduling conflicts. But what about December 30? Since December 31 is removed, December 30 would be the last day of the year, which is a Saturday. So, it's part of the week, so their birthday would still fall on a Saturday every year, which is consistent. So, only those with birthdays on December 31 would have issues. But the option includes both 30 and 31, so maybe the answer is B.\n\nWait, but the question is about which group would have continued scheduling conflicts. So, if the proposal is implemented, the main issue is that the last day is removed, so December 31 isn't part of any week. So, people with birthdays on December 31 would have their birthday on a day that's not part of any week, which might cause issues because it's not a regular day of the week. For example, if their birthday is on December 31, which is not a Monday, Tuesday, etc., they might not get a day off or it might not be recognized as a weekend. So, their scheduling could be disrupted because their birthday isn't aligned with the weekly cycle.\n\nOn the other hand, option D is about people who have three-day breaks when holidays are on Mondays or Fridays. If the calendar is fixed, then holidays would always fall on the same day of the week, so the three-day breaks would still occur as usual. So, maybe this group wouldn't have conflicts.\n\nOption A: If the weeks are fixed, then every seventh day is consistent, so their observances would align every year, so no conflicts.\n\nOption C: Planning events several years ahead would be easier because the days of the week are fixed, so no conflicts.\n\nSo, the most likely group to have continued scheduling conflicts would be option B, people with birthdays on December 30 or 31. But wait, December 30 would still be part of the week, so only December 31 would be an issue. But the option includes both, so maybe the answer is B.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is that the last day is removed, so the year effectively ends on December 30, making December 31 a non-day. So, people with birthdays on December 31 would have their birthday on a day that's not part of any week, causing scheduling issues. So, the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
