      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nThe editorial says that some people have suggested hiring private, for-profit companies to supply clean drinking water to areas where it's unavailable. But the editorial argues against this, stating that water shouldn't be supplied by private companies because clean water is essential for human health, and the purpose of a private company is to make a profit, not to promote health.\n\nThe question is asking which principle, if valid, would help justify the reasoning in the editorial. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nAlright, so I need to break down the editorial's argument. The main point is that private companies shouldn't supply water because their primary goal is profit, not health. The editorial is essentially saying that since water is crucial for health, it shouldn't be handled by entities whose main purpose isn't health promotion.\n\nSo, the reasoning seems to be: because something is essential for health, it should be provided by an organization whose main goal is health, not profit. That makes sense because if the organization's main goal is profit, they might prioritize cost-cutting or other profit-driven decisions over ensuring the water is safe and accessible.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: \"The mere fact that something actually promotes human health is not sufficient to show that its purpose is to promote health.\" Hmm, this seems to be pointing out that just because something promotes health doesn't mean that's its purpose. But in the editorial, the argument is more about the purpose of the company, not about whether the action promotes health. So I'm not sure if this directly supports the editorial's reasoning.\n\nOption B: \"Drinking water should never be supplied by an organization that is not able to consistently supply clean, safe water.\" This is more about the capability of the organization rather than their purpose. The editorial doesn't argue that private companies can't supply clean water; it's more about their motive being profit. So B might not be the best fit.\n\nOption C: \"If something is necessary for human health, then it should be provided by an organization whose primary purpose is the promotion of health.\" This seems to directly align with the editorial's reasoning. The editorial is saying that because clean water is essential for health, it should be provided by an organization (like a non-profit or government) whose main goal is health, not profit. So this principle would support the editorial's argument.\n\nOption D: \"A private company should not be allowed to supply a commodity that is essential to human health unless that commodity is also supplied by a government agency.\" This introduces the idea of government involvement as a condition. The editorial doesn't mention anything about government agencies; it's more about the purpose of the company. So D might not be the best fit either.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the editorial's reasoning is that because clean water is essential for health, it should be provided by an organization whose primary purpose is health promotion. That matches option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
