      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything correctly.\n\nThe context says there are three judgments that are known to be true:\n\n1. Either A is on a business trip, or B is on a business trip, or C is on a business trip. So, at least one of them must be on a trip.\n\n2. If A is on a business trip, then C is also on a business trip. So, A implies C.\n\n3. C can't leave because of his busy work. Hmm, this probably means that C is not on a business trip. Because if he's busy with work, he can't go on a trip.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options can be derived from this information. The options are A, B, C, D, each describing different scenarios about A and B's business trip statuses.\n\nLet me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, from judgment 3, C can't leave because of his busy work. So, C is not on a business trip. That means C is not going. So, C is not on a trip.\n\nNow, looking at judgment 2: If A is on a trip, then C must also be on a trip. But we just established that C is not on a trip. So, if A were on a trip, that would require C to be on a trip, which contradicts judgment 3. Therefore, A cannot be on a trip. Because if A were, C would have to be, but C isn't. So, A is not on a trip.\n\nSo, A is not on a trip, and C is not on a trip. Now, looking at judgment 1: Either A, B, or C is on a trip. Since A and C are not, that leaves B. So, B must be on a trip.\n\nSo, putting it all together: A is not on a trip, B is on a trip, and C is not on a trip.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) A is on business while B is not. But we just concluded that B is on a trip, so this can't be right.\n\nB) B is on business while A is not. That matches our conclusion: B is on a trip, A is not.\n\nC) Both A and B are on business. But A isn't, so this is wrong.\n\nD) Neither A nor B is on business. But B is on a trip, so this is also wrong.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
