      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options is not an example of avoidance conditioning based on the given context. Let me start by understanding what avoidance conditioning is. From the context, it's when someone reacts to a situation that causes disgust or unhappiness by escaping it, and then this reaction becomes more likely in similar situations later.\n\nSo, avoidance conditioning involves learning to avoid a situation that leads to an unpleasant experience. The key here is that the behavior is learned to escape an aversive stimulus, and this behavior increases in similar contexts.\n\nNow, let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Detour after seeing garbage on the road. If someone sees garbage and decides to take a detour, that's avoiding the unpleasant sight or smell. So, this is likely avoidance conditioning because they're escaping an aversive situation, and they might do this again in the future when they encounter similar garbage.\n\nOption B: When you hit something hot, quickly withdraw your hand. This seems like a reflex action, not a learned behavior. When you touch something hot, your body automatically pulls away to avoid pain. This is more of a reflex (like a withdrawal reflex) rather than a learned avoidance. So, this might not be avoidance conditioning.\n\nOption C: Leave temporarily when you feel noisy in the house. If someone leaves a noisy environment to avoid the discomfort, that's an example of avoidance conditioning. They're escaping an aversive stimulus (noise) and are likely to do this again when the environment becomes noisy.\n\nOption D: Afraid to meet strangers and dare not go to the streets. This seems like a learned behavior where the person avoids situations that cause fear or anxiety. They might have had a bad experience with strangers before, so they avoid going out. This is definitely avoidance conditioning because they're escaping an aversive situation and are more likely to avoid it in the