      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem about comets and their mass estimation. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nThe context says that comets don't emit their own light; they reflect light from other sources, like the Sun. Scientists estimate the mass of comets based on their brightness. The idea is that a more massive comet reflects more light, so the brighter it is, the more massive they think it is.\n\nThen, there's this satellite probe that found something about Halley's comet. It says that the material of Halley's comet reflects 60 times less light per unit of mass than what was previously thought. Hmm, that's a bit tricky. So, per unit of mass, the reflectivity is much lower than before. \n\nLet me break this down. If the material reflects less light per unit mass, that means for the same amount of mass, it's not reflecting as much light as scientists originally believed. So, if a comet's brightness is used to estimate its mass, and the reflectivity is lower, then the same brightness would imply a higher mass. Because if the material isn't reflecting as much, you need more mass to produce the same amount of reflected light.\n\nWait, let me think again. Suppose the reflectivity is 60 times less. So, if before they thought each unit of mass reflects X amount of light, now it's X/60. So, if the comet's brightness is the same as before, but each unit of mass contributes less light, then to get the same brightness, the comet must have more mass. Because brightness is proportional to mass times reflectivity. So, if reflectivity is lower, mass must be higher to keep brightness the same.\n\nSo, the previous estimates were based on a higher reflectivity. Now that we know it's 60 times less, the actual mass must be higher than what was previously thought. Because the same brightness would require more mass if each unit reflects less light.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) The reflective properties vary from comet to comet. The context only talks about Halley's comet, so this might not be supported. It's possible, but the info doesn't directly support this statement.\n\nB) Previous estimates of Halley's mass were too low. That makes sense because if the reflectivity is lower, the same brightness implies a higher mass. So, previous estimates, which assumed higher reflectivity, would have underestimated the mass.\n\nC) The total light reflected is less than thought. Wait, the context says the material reflects less per unit mass, but the total light depends on both the mass and the reflectivity. If the mass is higher, the total light might actually be the same or even more. Because if the mass is 60 times higher, and the reflectivity is 60 times lower, the total light would be the same. So, the total light reflected might not be less. So, this option might not be correct.\n\nD) Scientists need more info. The context provides new info that changes the estimate, so maybe they can adjust their calculations. It doesn't necessarily say they need more info; they just need to recalculate using the new reflectivity.\n\nSo, the most supported statement is B: Previous estimates were too low because the reflectivity is lower, so the mass must be higher to account for the same brightness.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
