s whether option B is internal or external. If the operator is internal, then it's not outsourcing. If external, it is. The option doesn't specify, but the term \"operator\" might refer to an internal department. So option B might not be outsourcing because it's the company's own operator making improvements, not an external company.\n\nWait, but the definition says the company entrusts all or part of the operations to an external company. So if the operator is external, then it's outsourcing. But if it's internal, it's not. Since the option doesn't specify, but the question is asking which does NOT belong, I think option B is the one that doesn't fit because it's about the operator (possibly internal) improving the system, not outsourcing to an external company.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
