about resources, not about the effectiveness of computer simulations versus lab experiments. So this might not weaken the argument much.\n\nOption D: University students can learn effectively without lab experience. Oh, this is interesting. If this is true, then the main concern of the argument\u2014that students don't know how to use lab equipment in university\u2014is addressed. If they can learn effectively without that experience, then the argument's premise that the trend is bad because it leads to unprepared students is weakened. So this directly challenges the conclusion by showing that the supposed negative outcome isn't actually a problem.\n\nSo putting it all together, the argument's main point is that without lab experience, students are unprepared. If option D is true, it means that being unprepared isn't a problem because they can still learn effectively. Therefore, option D weakens the argument the most.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
