ecessarily about the time frame. However, if the device can help locate people quickly, it could make a difference in rescue operations, especially in the critical first hours. So this might be a point in favor, but I'm not sure if it's the strongest.\n\nOption C: Extensive training is required to operate the device effectively. This seems like a potential weakness. If the device requires a lot of training, it might not be as useful in emergency situations where time is of the essence. Emergency teams might not have the luxury of time to train extensively. So this could actually weaken the argument rather than strengthen it. Therefore, this option is probably not the correct answer.\n\nOption D: The device gives a distinctive reading when the signals it detects come from human beings rather than from any other living beings. This is interesting because it means the device can differentiate between human signals and those from other animals or sources. This specificity would be crucial in a rescue scenario. If the device can accurately identify human signals, it reduces the chances of false positives or distractions, making it more reliable for emergency teams. This directly supports the argument by showing that the device is not only effective at detecting signals but also at distinguishing the relevant ones, which would make it more useful in real-world applications.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option D seems to be the strongest because it addresses a potential flaw in the argument\u2014that the device might detect signals from other sources, which could be a problem. By specifying that it can distinguish human signals, it makes the device more reliable and thus strengthens the argument.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
