nt doesn't say that all machines need special detergents, just front-loaders because of the water issue.\n\nOption C: A laundry detergent formulated especially for front-loading washers dissolves more readily in them than it does in top-loading washers.\n\nThis is about the special detergent dissolving better in front-loaders. The argument does say that regular detergent doesn't dissolve well, so the special one must. But does the argument assume that the special detergent dissolves more readily in front-loaders than in top-loaders? Or just that it dissolves better in front-loaders than regular detergent does? The argument doesn't compare front and top loaders in terms of the special detergent's performance. It just says that the special detergent works in front-loaders. So maybe this isn't the assumption, because the argument doesn't make that comparison.\n\nOption D: Washers that use more water get clothes cleaner than those that use less.\n\nThis is about water usage and cleanliness. The argument says front-loaders use less water, which causes the detergent not to dissolve. But it doesn't say that more water makes things cleaner. It's possible that more water could help, but the argument is about the detergent's solubility, not the amount of water itself. So this might not be the assumption.\n\nSo, putting it together, the argument relies on the idea that for a detergent to clean clothes, it needs to dissolve. Without dissolving, it can't clean. Therefore, the assumption is that a detergent must dissolve to clean effectively. That's option A.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The argument says that because the detergent doesn't dissolve, you need a special one. So the link is that dissolving is necessary for cleaning. So yes, the assumption is that without dissolving, the detergent can't clean, which is what option A states.\n\nOption C is about the special detergent dissolving better in front-loaders than in top-loaders, but the argument doesn't compare the two.