gree to C, which is taking the road of reform. So B is saying that they can't agree not to take it, which is the same as saying they must agree to take it. So B is also correct.\n\nWait, but can both A and B be correct? Let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are A, B, C, D. The question is which can be deduced. So perhaps both A and B are correct, but let me see.\n\nWait, the original context says that without C, you can't reform R, which is necessary for P, which is necessary for S. So S requires C. So A is correct.\n\nB says that those who advocate S cannot agree not to take the road of reform. So if someone is for S, they must be for C. So B is also correct. Hmm, but maybe the options are exclusive, so perhaps only one is correct.\n\nWait, let me think again. The contrapositive of S \u2192 C is \u00acC \u2192 \u00acS. So if someone doesn't take the road of reform (\u00acC), they can't strengthen S. So if someone advocates S, they must agree to C. So B is correct.\n\nWait, but the options are A, B, C, D. So both A and B seem correct. But maybe I'm misunderstanding the options.\n\nWait, let me look at the options again.\n\nOption A: To strengthen S, we must establish C. That's S \u2192 C, which is correct.\n\nOption B: Those who advocate S cannot agree not to take the road of reform. So if someone is for S, they can't be against C. That's the same as S \u2192 C, so B is also correct.\n\nWait, but in logic, if S \u2192 C, then both A and B are correct. So perhaps both A and B are correct, but looking at the options, maybe only one is correct. Let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are A, B, C, D. So perhaps both A and B are correct, but the question is which can be deduced. So maybe both A and B are correct, but perhaps the answer expects only one. Alternatively, perhaps I'm missing something.\n\nWait, let me think again. The context says that without C, you can't reform R, which is necessary for P, which is necessary for S. So S requires