      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. The context given is a statement: \"If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.\" And I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is closest in meaning to this statement.\n\nFirst, I should break down the original statement. It seems to be a conditional statement. In logic, conditional statements are often structured as \"If P, then Q.\" So, in this case, P is \"closing all the wrong doors,\" and Q is \"truth will be shut out.\" So, the statement is P \u2192 Q.\n\nNow, I need to understand what each of the options is saying and see which one matches the original statement or its contrapositive, because in logic, the contrapositive of P \u2192 Q is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, which is logically equivalent.\n\nLet's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.\" This sounds like Q \u2192 P. So, if Q happens, then P must have happened. But in logic, the contrapositive is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, not Q \u2192 P. So, this is not the contrapositive; it's the converse. The converse isn't necessarily equivalent to the original statement. So, A might not be the correct answer.\n\nOption B: \"Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\" Hmm, this is a bit tricky. Let me parse it. \"Cannot be shut out unless...\" So, unless you close all the wrong doors, truth can't be shut out. That sounds like if you don't close all the wrong doors, truth won't be shut out. So, in logical terms, that would be \u00acP \u2192 \u00acQ. Wait, but the original statement is P \u2192 Q. So, \u00acP \u2192 \u00acQ is the inverse of the original statement, which isn't equivalent. Alternatively, maybe I'm misinterpreting. Let me think again. \"Truth cannot be shut out unless all the wrong doors are closed.\" So, to shut out truth, you must close all wrong doors. That would mean that closing all wrong doors is a necessary condition for truth to be shut out. So, in other words, if truth is shut out, then all wrong doors must have been closed. That's Q \u2192 P, which is the converse again. So, similar to option A, this is the converse, not the contrapositive. So, B might not be correct either.\n\nOption C: \"If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\" Let's translate this. \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP. That's the contrapositive of the original statement P \u2192 Q. Because the contrapositive is formed by negating both and switching them. So, if the original is P \u2192 Q, the contrapositive is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP. So, this matches exactly with option C. Therefore, C is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\nOption D: \"To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.\" This seems a bit more abstract. It's saying that accepting truth requires rejecting wrong. But the original statement is about closing wrong doors leading to truth being shut out. So, this is more about a necessary condition for accepting truth, which might not directly map to the original conditional. It's a bit vague, but I don't think it's the closest in meaning.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The original statement is about closing wrong doors causing truth to be shut out. So, if you don't close all wrong doors, then truth isn't shut out. That's the inverse, but the contrapositive is if truth isn't shut out, then not all wrong doors are closed. Which is exactly what option C says. So, C is the contrapositive and thus logically equivalent.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct answer should be option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
