      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D correctly applies the definition of division provided in the context. Let me start by understanding the definitions given.\n\nThe context says that division is a logical method where a concept is divided into several smaller categories. It's about splitting a broader concept into specific, non-overlapping subcategories. On the other hand, decomposition is about breaking an object into parts, but the parts don't have a species relationship with the whole. So, division is about categorizing, while decomposition is about breaking into parts without the part-whole hierarchy.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: The definition is divided into defined items, defined items, and defined joint items. Wait, that seems repetitive. Defined items are mentioned twice, which doesn't make sense. Maybe it's a typo, but even if it's not, dividing a definition into parts like that doesn't seem to fit the division definition because it's not clear if these are subcategories or parts. It might be decomposition instead.\n\nOption B: Hohhot is divided into districts like Xincheng, Huimin, Sihan, and Yuquan. Hohhot is a city, and these are its administrative districts. So, this is breaking down a whole (the city) into its parts (districts). According to the context, this is decomposition, not division, because it's about parts of a whole, not categorizing a concept into subcategories.\n\nOption C: Celestial bodies are divided into natural and man-made. This seems like a proper division. Natural celestial bodies include stars, planets, etc., while man-made includes satellites, space stations. These are two distinct, non-overlapping categories under the broader concept of celestial bodies. So this fits the division definition.\n\nOption D: Penalty is divided into principal punishment, deprivation of political rights, confiscation of property, etc. Wait, principal punishment is a category, but deprivation of political rights and confiscation of property are additional penalties. I think in legal terms, principal punishment is the main type, and the others are additional. So, this might not be a proper division because the additional penalties are not subcategories of the main concept but rather separate types. It might be more of a list rather than a division into species.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- A: Repetitive and unclear, possibly decomposition.\n- B: Decomposition of a city into districts.\n- C: Proper division into natural and man-made.\n- D: Not a clear division, more like a list with a main category and additional ones.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
