ument assumes that approving of oneself leads to a virtuous life.\n\nOption C: Attaining happiness is the real goal of people who strive for material well-being. The context doesn't talk about why people strive for material well-being. It just contrasts the good life as virtuous rather than material. So C might not be an assumption because the argument doesn't address the goals of people seeking material well-being.\n\nOption D: Approval of one's own character and projects tends not to result in pleasurable feelings. The context says happiness isn't about pleasurable feelings but about approval. So, does approval not lead to pleasure? Or is it that happiness is separate from pleasure? The argument doesn't say that approval doesn't result in pleasure; it just says happiness isn't about pleasure. So D might not be an assumption because the argument doesn't claim that approval doesn't bring pleasure, just that happiness isn't defined by pleasure.\n\nSo, putting it together, the argument assumes that approving of one's character and projects leads to a morally virtuous life, which is option B. The other options either go beyond what's stated or aren't directly supported by the argument.\n</think>\n\nThe argument connects happiness to approving of one's character and projects, which in turn defines the good life as morally virtuous. Option B directly aligns with this by stating that such approval leads to a morally virtuous life, making it the required assumption.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
