 argument is about the fee's impact on consumers. So this might be a weakness, but I'm not sure if it's the most serious. Let me keep this in mind.\n\nOption C: Increasing the cost of disposing of an appliance properly increases the incentive to dispose of it improperly. Oh, this is interesting. If the fee is charged at salvage time, making it more expensive to dispose properly, people might be more inclined to dispose of appliances improperly, like throwing them away illegally instead of paying the fee. That would mean that the fee doesn't actually reduce waste because people are just finding other ways to get rid of their appliances without salvaging. This directly undermines the argument's conclusion that the fee would reduce waste by making people keep their appliances longer. So this seems like a strong candidate for weakening the argument.\n\nOption D: For people who have bought new appliances recently, the salvage fee would not need to be paid for a number of years. Wait, if the fee is imposed when the appliance is first sold, then when they discard it later, they don't have to pay again. But the argument is about imposing the fee at salvage time. So if the fee is paid upfront, then when they discard, they don't have to pay again. But the argument says that imposing the fee at salvage time would make people keep appliances longer. If the fee is already paid when they buy it, then when they discard, they don't have to pay again, so the incentive to keep it longer wouldn't be there. Wait, no, the argument is that if the fee is imposed at salvage time, people would keep their appliances longer. But if the fee is paid upfront, then when they discard, they don't have to pay again, so the fee doesn't act as a deterrent at the time of discarding. So this would mean that the fee doesn't have the intended effect because it's already been paid. Therefore, this would weaken the argument because it shows that the fee doesn't function as intended when imposed upfront. So 