      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best supports Lao Liu's plan. Let me start by understanding the context.\n\nLao Li mentioned that while college enrollments are decreasing each year, primary school enrollments are increasing a lot. Because of this, the district education department suggested building a new primary school. But Lao Liu proposed an alternative solution: using classrooms from some colleges and universities as primary school classrooms.\n\nSo, Lao Liu's plan is to repurpose underused college classrooms for primary schools instead of building a new school. The question is asking which option supports this plan the most.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Classrooms in some colleges and universities are not suitable to be used as primary school classrooms.\" Hmm, if the classrooms aren't suitable, that would actually weaken Lao Liu's plan. Because if they're not suitable, then using them wouldn't be a good solution. So this doesn't support his plan; it undermines it.\n\nOption B: \"The cost of building a university is much higher than that of building a primary school.\" Wait, Lao Liu's plan is about repurposing existing classrooms, not about building new universities or primary schools. So the cost comparison here might not directly support his plan. It's more about the cost of construction, but Lao Liu is suggesting using existing spaces, so maybe this isn't the best support.\n\nOption C: \"Although the birth rate has not increased, the number of families with children studying in colleges and universities has increased a lot.\" Hmm, if more families have kids in colleges, does that mean more classrooms are being used? Or maybe it's the opposite. Wait, if the birth rate hasn't increased, but more families have children in colleges, perhaps that means more students are going to colleges, but the context says college enrollments are decreasing. Wait, that's confusing. Let me think again. The context says college enrollments are decreasing, so maybe the number of students in colleges is going down. If more families have children in colleges, but enrollments are down, that might mean that each family has more children in college, but overall numbers are decreasing. Not sure how this directly supports Lao Liu's plan. Maybe it's about the availability of classrooms. If more families have children in colleges, but enrollments are down, perhaps the colleges have more empty classrooms. So this could support the idea that there are underused classrooms available.\n\nOption D: \"Even before the number of colleges and universities decreased, there were many colleges and universities whose classrooms were rarely used.\" Oh, this seems relevant. If even before the decrease in enrollments, there were colleges with rarely used classrooms, that means there are already available spaces that could be repurposed. This directly supports Lao Liu's plan because it shows that there are classrooms that aren't being used, making it feasible to use them for primary schools.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Option A weakens the plan.\n- Option B is about construction costs, which isn't directly related to repurposing.\n- Option C might suggest more families have kids in colleges, but with decreasing enrollments, perhaps more classrooms are free, but it's a bit indirect.\n- Option D clearly states that there were already underused classrooms before the decrease, which directly supports the feasibility of Lao Liu's plan.\n\nTherefore, the best support is Option D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
