      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context is that someone from HR says, \"Due to the limited recruitment positions in our company, it is impossible for all candidates to be hired in this recruitment examination.\" So, the main point here is that not everyone can be hired because there aren't enough positions.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options can lead to that conclusion. So, I need to find which statement necessarily results in the conclusion that not all candidates can be hired.\n\nLet's look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: \"In this recruitment examination, some candidates may be hired.\" Hmm, \"may be\" implies possibility, not certainty. The HR person is stating that it's impossible for all to be hired, which suggests that some won't be. But Option A is talking about some possibly being hired, which doesn't directly lead to the conclusion that some won't be. It's more about the possibility of hiring, not the necessity of not hiring everyone.\n\nOption B: \"In this recruitment examination, there may be candidates who will not be hired.\" Again, \"may be\" is about possibility. So, it's saying that it's possible that some won't be hired. But the HR person is stating a certainty\u2014that it's impossible for all to be hired, meaning that some definitely won't be. So, Option B is about possibility, not certainty, so it doesn't necessarily lead to the conclusion.\n\nOption C: \"In this recruitment examination, there are bound to be candidates who will not be hired.\" \"Bound to be\" means it's certain. This aligns with the HR person's statement that it's impossible for all to be hired, so some must not be hired. This seems to fit because if it's impossible for all to be hired, then it's certain that some won't be.\n\nOption D: \"In this recruitment examination, there must be candidates to be hired.\" \"Must be\" implies that hiring is certain, but the HR person's statement doesn't necessarily say that any candidates will be hired. It just says that not all can be. So, it's possible that none are hired, but that's probably not the case. However, the HR person's statement doesn't confirm that any will be hired, just that not all can. So, Option D might not be the correct lead because the conclusion is about the impossibility of hiring all, not the necessity of hiring some.\n\nWait, but actually, if it's impossible for all to be hired, does that mean that at least some must not be hired? Yes, that's correct. So, the conclusion is that some won't be hired, which is a certainty. So, Option C is the one that leads to that conclusion because it states that there are bound to be candidates who won't be hired.\n\nLet me double-check. The HR person's statement is a universal negative: not all can be hired. In logic, this is equivalent to saying that at least one cannot be hired. So, the correct conclusion is that some won't be hired, which is a certainty, not a possibility. Therefore, Option C is the correct one because it uses \"bound to be,\" which is a certainty, whereas Options A and B use \"may,\" which is possibility, and Option D talks about hiring, not not hiring.\n\nSo, the answer should be Option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
