 might discuss how parents can contribute, either through their own practices or by viewing courses rationally.\n\nOption B talks about parents' practice in family education, which aligns with the idea that daily life and family environment are key. Option C suggests parents should view courses rationally, which also makes sense because the context mentions that EQ classes are popular but might be misunderstood. Option D is about improving parents' EQ, which is related but perhaps a bit more indirect.\n\nThe context mentions that parents' role is crucial, so the next point might be about how parents can effectively practice EQ in their family education. Therefore, option B seems likely. Alternatively, option C could be about parents not relying solely on courses but understanding their role better.\n\nWait, the context says that EQ training can't replace daily life, so maybe the next point is about parents not over-relying on courses but focusing on their own practices. So, option C suggests viewing courses rationally, which would make sense as a follow-up. Alternatively, option B is about the practice itself.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit torn between B and C. Let me think again. The context ends by emphasizing the importance of parents and daily life. So, the next point could be about how parents should approach EQ training, perhaps by not just relying on courses but integrating it into daily life. So, option C, which says parents should view courses rationally, might be the next point. Alternatively, option B is about the practice in family education, which is more about the action parents should take.\n\nWait, the context says that EQ training can't replace daily life, so maybe the next point is about parents needing to improve their own EQ to better help their children. That would be option D. But the context doesn't explicitly mention that parents themselves need EQ training, just that they play a crucial role.\n\nAlternatively, the context might be leading into the idea that parents should be cautious about the courses, not just blindly following the trend. So, option C would be about viewing the courses rationally, which fits because the context mentions that the connotation of EQ has been alienated, meaning people's understanding is off. So, parents should be rational about the courses, not just follow the trend.\n\nI think I'm leaning towards option C because the context talks about the alienation of EQ's meaning and the importance of daily practice over training. So, the next point would be advising parents to be rational about these courses, not just enroll their kids without understanding the real value.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
