      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it. The context is about deep tillage and its effects on topsoil. It says that deep tillage is even worse for the world's topsoil supply than previously thought. The example given is that farmers who till deeply are ten times more likely to lose topsoil to erosion compared to those who use no-till methods. The conclusion is that farmers who currently till deeply should switch to no-till methods using other aeration techniques.\n\nNow, the question is asking which assumption the argument depends on. The options are A, B, C, D. I need to figure out which one is the underlying assumption that the argument relies on.\n\nLet me break it down. The argument is saying that because deep tillage leads to more erosion, farmers should switch to no-till. So, the argument is making a cause-and-effect claim: deep tillage causes more erosion, so changing methods would reduce erosion.\n\nTo find the assumption, I need to think about what the argument is taking for granted. An assumption is something that isn't explicitly stated but is necessary for the argument to hold.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: In deep-tillage farming, the deeper one tills, the greater the susceptibility to topsoil erosion. The context says that deep tillage is worse, but it doesn't specify whether deeper tilling within deep tillage methods causes more erosion. It just compares deep tillage to no-till. So, the argument doesn't assume that deeper is worse within deep tillage; it just says deep tillage is worse than no-till. So A might not be the assumption.\n\nOption B: Topsoil erosion does not make farmers want to till more deeply. Hmm, the argument is about farmers who already till deeply. It doesn't address whether erosion causes them to till more. The argument assumes that the problem is deep tillage leading to erosion, not that erosion leads to more tillage. So B is about a potential cycle, but the argument doesn't rely on that. It just says deep tillage causes erosion, so switch methods. So B might be an assumption, but I'm not sure yet.\n\nOption C: The most expensive farming methods employ topsoil aeration techniques other than deep tillage. The argument doesn't mention cost. It's about the effectiveness of no-till in reducing erosion. So the argument doesn't assume anything about the cost of methods. Therefore, C is probably not the assumption.\n\nOption D: Tilling by any method other than deep tillage is not a viable option. Wait, the argument suggests using other aeration techniques instead of deep tillage. So it assumes that there are viable alternatives. If D says that any other method isn't viable, that would contradict the argument. Because the argument is saying farmers should switch to other methods, implying those methods are viable. So the argument assumes that other methods are viable, meaning D is the opposite of what the argument assumes. Therefore, D is not the assumption.\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument says farmers should use other aeration techniques instead of deep tillage. So it's assuming that these other techniques are possible or viable. So the assumption is that there are viable alternatives. Therefore, the argument depends on the assumption that other methods are viable. So if D says that any other method isn't viable, that would be the opposite. So the correct assumption is that other methods are viable, which is not D. So D is not the assumption.\n\nWait, maybe I got confused. Let me rephrase. The argument is saying that farmers should switch to no-till methods using other aeration techniques. So it's assuming that these other techniques are available and effective. So the assumption is that these alternatives are viable. Therefore, the argument depends on the assumption that other methods are viable, which is the opposite of D. So D is not the assumption.\n\nWait, but the option