the argument assumes that the only reason farmers till deeply is because they don't know about the erosion, but that's not an option.\n\nWait, perhaps the assumption is that the farmers can switch without any issues, meaning that other methods are viable. So the argument assumes that no-till is a viable alternative. So the assumption is that other methods are viable, which would mean that option D is the opposite. So the argument depends on the assumption that other methods are viable, which is not D. So perhaps the correct answer is B.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try another approach. The argument is: deep tillage causes more erosion, so switch to no-till. The conclusion is that farmers should switch. For this conclusion to hold, the argument must assume that switching is possible and beneficial. So the assumption is that no-till is a viable alternative. But none of the options state that directly. So perhaps the assumption is that other methods are viable, which is not an option. Alternatively, the argument assumes that the increased likelihood of erosion is due to deep tillage and not other factors. But that's not an option either.\n\nWait, looking back at the options, option B says that topsoil erosion does not make farmers want to till more deeply. So the argument assumes that erosion isn't causing farmers to till more, otherwise, the problem would be a cycle. So the argument depends on the assumption that erosion doesn't lead to more tillage, which is option B.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the argument doesn't need to assume that. Because the argument is about the farmers who are already tilling deeply. It's not about whether erosion causes them to till more, but rather that deep tillage is the cause of erosion, so they should switch. So perhaps the assumption is that other methods are viable, which is not an option. So maybe the correct answer is B.\n\nWait, I'm going in circles. Let me try to outline the argument structure.\n\nPremise: Deep tillage lead