      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe university administrator says that any new department proposal won't be funded if two conditions are met: first, if there are fewer than 50 people available for hire per year in that field, and second, if the new department duplicates more than 25% of the material covered in an existing department. \n\nBut then, the administrator mentions that the proposed Area Studies Department does duplicate more than 25% of the material from the existing Anthropology Department. Despite this duplication, they say they will fund the new department. \n\nSo, the question is asking which statement logically follows from the administrator's statements. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break this down. The administrator's rule is that a proposal won't be funded if both conditions are true: fewer than 50 hires per year AND duplicates more than 25% of an existing department. \n\nIn this case, the Area Studies Department does duplicate more than 25% of Anthropology. But they are still funding it. That means that at least one of the two conditions must not be met. Since duplication is over 25%, the other condition must not be true. The other condition is that there are fewer than 50 people available for hire. So, if the department is being funded despite duplication, it must be that there are at least 50 people available for hire in the field of Area Studies. \n\nLet me look at the options:\n\nA) If the proposed area studies department did not duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in anthropology, then the new department would not be funded.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. The administrator's rule is that if both conditions are met, it won't be funded. But if duplication is less than or equal to 25%, then the duplication condition isn't met, so the department could still be funded regardless of the number of hires. So A is saying that if duplication is not over 25%, then it won't be funded, which isn't necessarily true. Because if duplication is under 25%, the department might still be funded if there are enough hires. So A is not necessarily true.\n\nB) The proposed area studies department would not duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in any existing department other than anthropology.\n\nHmm, the administrator only mentions duplication with Anthropology. They don't say anything about other departments. So B is making a claim about other departments, which isn't supported by the given information. So B is not necessarily true.\n\nC) The field of area studies has fewer than 50 people per year available for hire.\n\nWait, but the department is being funded despite duplication. So for the funding to happen, at least one condition must not be met. Since duplication is over 25%, the other condition (fewer than 50 hires) must not be true. So the number of hires must be at least 50. Therefore, C is the opposite of what's true. So C is incorrect.\n\nD) The field of area studies has at least 50 people per year available for hire.\n\nYes, this makes sense. Since the department is being funded despite duplication, the number of hires must be 50 or more. So D is the correct statement.\n\nLet me double-check. The rule is: not funded if (fewer than 50 hires AND duplicates >25%). The department duplicates >25%, but it's still funded. So the only way that can happen is if the number of hires is >=50. Therefore, D must be true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
