ith time moves more slowly in the gravitational field. In the theory of relativity, time is no longer an external parameter, but an active participant in the composition of the universe.\"\n\nSo D is definitely correct because it's directly stated. As for A, the passage doesn't explicitly say that relativity develops man's understanding, but it implies that by contrasting it with Newton's view. So A is also correct.\n\nWait, but the question is which is the most accurate understanding. So perhaps both A and D are correct, but looking at the options, maybe only one is intended.\n\nWait, the options are A, B, C, D. Let me think again.\n\nThe passage says that in relativity, time is an active participant, unlike Newton's view. So D is correct because it states that Newton didn't think time was actively involved.\n\nOption A says relativity develops man's understanding, which is true because it's a new theory that changes the understanding from absolute to relative.\n\nBut the question is about the most accurate understanding of the passage. The passage is mainly contrasting Newton's absolute time with relativity's view. So both A and D are correct, but perhaps the question expects both, but since it's multiple choice, maybe only one is correct.\n\nWait, looking back, the passage says that in relativity, time is no longer an external parameter but an active participant. So D is correct because it's directly about Newton's view.\n\nOption A is also correct because relativity does develop our understanding beyond Newton's.\n\nBut perhaps the question is looking for the main point, which is that relativity changes the understanding, so A is correct. But D is also correct as a fact.\n\nWait, maybe I should look at the options again.\n\nOption A: The theory of relativity develops man's understanding of time. Yes, because it's a new theory that changes the view.\n\nOption D: Newton did not think that time was actively involved in the composition of the universe. Yes, because 