      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D has a reasoning pattern similar to the given argument. Let me start by understanding the original argument.\n\nThe original argument says: If Juan went to the party, it's highly unlikely Maria would have enjoyed it. But Maria did enjoy the party, so it's unlikely Juan was there. \n\nBreaking this down, it's a form of logical reasoning where the presence of one event (Juan attending) makes another (Maria enjoying) unlikely. Since the second event happened (Maria enjoyed), we infer that the first event (Juan attending) didn't happen. This seems like a contrapositive or modus tollens argument.\n\nNow, let's look at each option to see which one follows a similar structure.\n\nOption A: This year's election will probably be fair. But Popov probably won't win unless the election is unfair. So, Popov won't win.\n\nHmm, this is a bit different. It starts with the election being fair, then says Popov won't win unless it's unfair. So, if the election is fair, Popov doesn't win. But the conclusion is that Popov won't win. It's more of a direct implication rather than a contrapositive. Not sure yet.\n\nOption B: Kapinski is probably friendly because he sells insurance, and most insurance sellers are friendly.\n\nThis is an inductive argument based on generalization. It doesn't involve conditionals or contrapositives. So, probably not similar.\n\nOption C: If Clarissa missed the bus, she's unlikely to get to work on time. Since she was at work on time, it's unlikely she missed the bus.\n\nThis looks similar to the original argument. The structure is: If P, then not Q. Q happened, so not P. That's exactly the same as the original. So this seems like a match.\n\nOption D: If the lottery were fair, the winner wouldn't have been likely to win. Since the person would have been likely to win if it were unfair, the lottery was probably unfair.\n\nThis is a bit more complex. It uses two conditionals. The first is if fair, then unlikely to win. The second is if unfair, then likely to win. The conclusion is that since the person was likely to win, the lottery was unfair. This is a bit different because it's using two conditional statements and drawing a conclusion based on both, whereas the original only uses one conditional and its contrapositive.\n\nSo, comparing all options, option C has the same structure as the original argument. Both use a conditional statement, then observe the opposite of the consequent, leading to the negation of the antecedent. That's modus tollens reasoning.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
