s more about who attends, not why they must attend.\n\nOption C: Even if you attend an English tutoring class, you may not pass the exam. This actually weakens the argument because it introduces doubt about the effectiveness of attending the class. So this isn't helpful for strengthening.\n\nOption D: If you do not attend the English tutoring class, you will not be able to pass the exam. This is a strong statement because it directly links not attending the class to failure. It supports the idea that attending is necessary because without it, you can't pass. This seems to directly strengthen the argument by providing a clear consequence of not attending.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the argument is that attending is necessary. Option D provides a conditional that if you don't attend, you can't pass, which logically supports the necessity. Therefore, D is the best option to strengthen the argument.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
