ording to his rule, he would have to vote for everyone who voted for Zhao Jie, including himself. So if Wang Ming voted for Zhao Jie, he would have to vote for himself as well. But does that create a loop? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Wang Ming didn't vote for Zhao Jie, so he didn't have to vote for anyone else. But that might not make sense because if someone else voted for Zhao Jie, Wang Ming would have to vote for them.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to model this with some logical statements.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let W be the set of people Wang Ming voted for.\n- Let L be the set of people Li Zilu voted for.\n- Let Z be the set of people Zhang Hao voted for, which is empty.\n\nFrom the context:\n\n1. Zhang Hao didn't vote for anyone: Z = empty set.\n2. Wang Ming checked all those who checked Zhao Jie: So if someone is in the set of people who voted for Zhao Jie, then Wang Ming voted for them. So W includes all people who voted for Zhao Jie.\n3. Li Zilu ticked all those who didn't get the full vote: So Li Zilu voted for everyone who didn't vote for all three candidates. So if someone didn't vote for all three, Li Zilu voted for them.\n\nWait, but the candidates are three, so each voter can vote for any subset of the three. So for each voter, if they didn't vote for all three, Li Zilu voted for them.\n\nSo Li Zilu's votes include everyone except those who voted for all three candidates.\n\nNow, let's think about each person's voting behavior.\n\nFirst, Zhang Hao didn't vote for anyone, so he didn't vote for any of the three candidates. So he didn't vote for himself, Wang Ming, or Li Zilu.\n\nNow, let's consider Wang Ming. He voted for all those who voted for Zhao Jie. So if someone voted for Zhao Jie, Wang Ming also voted for them.\n\nBut we don't know if Zhao Jie received any votes. However, since Zhang Hao didn't vote for anyone, Zhao Jie's votes must come from others, like Wang Ming or Li Zilu.\n\nWait, but if Wang Ming voted for Zhao Jie, then according