      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best emphasizes the argument presented in the context. Let me start by understanding the context thoroughly.\n\nThe context talks about TV ads that repeat the same words many times to make a deep impression on the audience so they remember the product. However, it's stated that this approach is unwise. Surveys show that businesses using such long-term ads have faced economic trouble with a backlog of products. So, the argument is that repeating ads doesn't achieve the desired effect and actually has a negative impact.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option best emphasizes this argument. Let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: It says that some viewers don't mind the repetition, but many think more about the art of advertising. Hmm, this seems to suggest that while some are okay, others prefer better advertising techniques. But the context is more about the negative effect of repetition leading to economic trouble, not just a preference for better art. So maybe not the best fit.\n\nOption B: This states that after a good TV program, any ad makes the audience impatient. Wait, the context doesn't mention anything about the quality of the TV program or impatience due to that. It's specifically about the repetition of ads causing negative effects. So B might not be directly related.\n\nOption C: It mentions that while the law doesn't prohibit repeating ad words, merchants should consider diverse audience feelings. The context does talk about the negative impact, but it doesn't bring up legal aspects or diverse feelings. It's more about the repetition leading to economic issues. So C might not be the best choice either.\n\nOption D: This says that repeated ads cause viewers to get bored or angry, leading to strong dislike for the products. That aligns well with the context. The context mentions that the ads didn't achieve their goal and had a negative effect, which would include viewers being annoyed and thus avoiding the product. So D seems to directly emphasize the argument.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says that the businesses have a backlog because the ads didn't work. So the negative effect is that people don't buy the product, possibly because they're annoyed by the ads. Option D directly links the repetition to viewer annoyance and antipathy towards the product, which fits perfectly.\n\nSo, after considering each option, D is the best choice because it clearly states the negative consequence of repeating ads, which is exactly what the context is arguing against.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
