      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D describes the self-depletion phenomenon based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding what self-depletion is. From the context, it's a phenomenon where individuals experience a decline in their ability or willingness to conduct voluntary activities over time. This includes things like controlling their environment, self-control, making choices, and initiating behaviors. Additionally, it mentions job burnout, self-depletion without progress.\n\nSo, self-depletion is about a long-term decline in motivation and ability to perform voluntary tasks, leading to burnout and a lack of progress. Now, I need to look at each option and see which one fits this description.\n\nOption A: Lee didn't finish his doctoral thesis and kept postponing it, resulting in not graduating for eight years. This seems like procrastination and lack of completion, but is it self-depletion? It might be more about poor time management or lack of motivation, but does it show a decline over time? Maybe, but I'm not sure yet.\n\nOption B: Chen has been a deputy director for 20 years, published some works, but hasn't been promoted. This sounds more like a lack of advancement despite tenure. It doesn't necessarily show a decline in his ability or willingness to perform tasks, just that he hasn't moved up. So maybe not self-depletion.\n\nOption C: Song has been doing sales for nearly ten years, changed companies, but average performance and no income change. This seems like a lack of progress in his career, but again, is it a decline in his ability or willingness? It could be, but I'm not certain. He might just be stuck in a rut without necessarily feeling depleted.\n\nOption D: Wang has been an archive administrator for 15 years and feels numb to his work, with no desire to change or get promoted. This sounds like job burnout. He's been in the same role for a long time and has lost motivation, which aligns with the self-depletion definition. He's not just not progressing; he's emotionally detached and unwilling to make changes.\n\nComparing all options, D seems to fit best because it directly mentions feeling numb and a lack of desire to change, which are signs of self-depletion and burnout. The others either don't show a decline over time or are more about external factors like promotions or performance without the internal depletion aspect.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
