ion.\n\nOption D: Sun's ability to distinguish colors is poor. The doctor found that this is a manifestation of color weakness. Color weakness, like colorblindness, affects the perception of color. So, Sun is perceiving the color attribute incorrectly. If he's seeing a real object as a whole correctly but misperceiving its color, this fits the definition. However, I need to think if color weakness is considered a disturbance of perceptive synthesis. The context says it's about perceiving the whole correctly but individual attributes wrong. So, if Sun can see the object as a whole but can't see the color correctly, that fits.\n\nWait, but let me compare B and D. Both seem to fit. Zhang is perceiving the pendulum as a whole but misperceiving its movement. Sun is perceiving objects as a whole but misperceiving their color. So both B and D could be correct. But the question asks which one belongs to the disturbance, so maybe only one is correct.\n\nWait, the context says \"perceiving a real thing correctly as an objective whole.\" So, in B, Zhang is perceiving the pendulum correctly as a pendulum but thinks it's stopped when it's moving. That's a misperception of an attribute (movement). In D, Sun can't distinguish colors, so he's perceiving the color attribute incorrectly. Both seem to fit.\n\nBut looking back, the options are A, B, C, D, and the correct answer is one of them. Let me think again. The context says the patient is correct as an objective whole but has inconsistent individual attributes. So, in B, the pendulum is correctly perceived as a pendulum, but its movement is misperceived. In D, the color is misperceived, but the object is still correctly perceived as a whole. So both B and D fit. But the options are single-choice, so perhaps only one is correct.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me check each option again.\n\nOption A: Hallucination, not perceiving a real thing, so no.\n\nOption B: Real pendulum, but misperceiving movement. Fits.\n\nOption 