      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context is about a class selecting outstanding graduates. The class leader says, \"Either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate.\" Then the Youth League Party Secretary says, \"I disagree.\"\n\nThe question is asking which option accurately expresses the party secretary's opinion. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the class leader is saying. The leader uses the word \"either,\" which in logic terms is an exclusive OR. That means either Li Xue is selected, or Wang Lei is selected, but not both. So, the leader is suggesting that only one of them can be an outstanding graduate.\n\nNow, the party secretary disagrees with this statement. So, the secretary is opposing the leader's position. In logic, when someone disagrees with a statement, they are asserting the negation of that statement.\n\nThe leader's statement is: Li Xue OR Wang Lei (but not both). So, in logical terms, that's L \u2228 W, but since it's exclusive, it's actually (L \u2227 \u00acW) \u2228 (\u00acL \u2227 W). But for the sake of simplicity, maybe I can treat it as L \u2228 W, but knowing that it's exclusive.\n\nWait, actually, in formal logic, \"either...or\" is typically exclusive, meaning only one can be true. So, the leader's statement is that exactly one of them is selected.\n\nThe party secretary disagrees, so the negation of the leader's statement would be that it's not the case that exactly one is selected. So, the negation would be that either both are selected or neither is selected. Because if the leader says only one, the opposite is that either both are in or both are out.\n\nSo, the party secretary's opinion is that either both Li Xue and Wang Lei are rated as outstanding graduates, or neither of them is. That would be the negation of the exclusive OR.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Both Li Xue and Wang Lei were rated as outstanding graduates. This is only part of the negation, but not the whole. Because the negation includes both possibilities: both are in or both are out.\n\nB) Neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei can be rated as outstanding graduates. Again, this is only part of the negation. It's the other possibility, but not the whole.\n\nC) Either Li Xue and Wang Lei are both rated as outstanding graduates, or neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei can be rated as outstanding graduates. This seems to cover both possibilities, which is the correct negation.\n\nD) Li Xue was rated as an outstanding graduate, Wang Lei cannot be rated as an outstanding graduate. This is actually the same as the leader's statement, just phrased differently. So, the secretary wouldn't agree with this.\n\nWait, no. The leader's statement is that either Li Xue is selected or Wang Lei is selected, but not both. So, the leader is saying that exactly one is selected. The secretary is disagreeing, so the secretary is saying that it's not the case that exactly one is selected. Therefore, the secretary is saying that either both are selected or neither is selected.\n\nSo, option C is the correct one because it captures both possibilities: both are selected or neither is selected.\n\nLet me double-check. The leader's statement is an exclusive OR: (L \u2228 W) \u2227 \u00ac(L \u2227 W). The negation of that would be \u00ac[(L \u2228 W) \u2227 \u00ac(L \u2227 W)] which simplifies to \u00ac(L \u2228 W) \u2228 (L \u2227 W). Which is equivalent to (\u00acL \u2227 \u00acW) \u2228 (L \u2227 W). So, that's exactly what option C says: either both are selected or neither is selected.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So, the correct answer should be option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
